= Biological cell
{wiki=Cell_(biology)}

= Cell
{synonym}

A <biological cell> is a membrane-bounded basic unit of living organization. In a cell-level experiment it can be an <experimental unit>, provided that treatment assignment and relevant interactions are handled at that level.

The shared architecture of cellular life combines a selective <cell membrane>, internal <cytoplasm>, inherited information in <DNA>, and <ribosomes> that assemble <proteins>. <Metabolism> supplies transformations of matter and <energy> needed for maintenance and growth. These components form a coupled system: a boundary retains useful <molecules>, <enzymes> organize <chemical reactions>, and inherited information supports rebuilding the machinery. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: prokaryotic cells].

A worked structural comparison: in a typical <prokaryote>, <DNA> occupies a region without a surrounding <cell nucleus>; in a typical nucleated <eukaryote>, much of the <DNA> lies inside a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus>. Both still have a <cell membrane>, <cytoplasm> and <ribosomes>. The extra boundary compartmentalizes processes; it does not create a different basic principle of living organization. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: eukaryotic cells].

\Image[cell-compartments.png]
{title=Shared cellular architecture with different DNA compartmentalization}
{description=Original simplified schematics of a <prokaryote> and a nucleated <eukaryote>. Colors identify corresponding components; sizes and component counts are illustrative, and additional structures are omitted.}

Shared architecture describes cellular life and its developmental machinery, rather than requiring every specialized <biological cell> to retain every component. A mature mammalian <red blood cell>, for example, lacks a <cell nucleus>, although it develops from nucleated precursors. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: components of blood]. This distinction makes cellular unity compatible with striking specialization.

= Cell viability
{parent=Biological cell}

= B cell
{parent=Biological cell}

An immune-cell lineage associated with antibody responses. Lineage-specific proliferation and clonal expansion should be distinguished from changes in a mixed sample.

= Cell death
{parent=Biological cell}

= Cell division
{parent=Biological cell}

= Divisome
{parent=Cell division}

The bacterial machinery assembling and remodeling the envelope at the division site.

= Somatic cell
{parent=Biological cell}

= Stress response
{parent=Biological cell}

= Natural killer cell
{parent=Biological cell}

An innate cytotoxic lymphocyte whose activation integrates inhibitory and activating signals. Reduced class-I display can remove inhibitory signalling, but is not by itself a universal guarantee of killing.

= Vesicle trafficking
{parent=Biological cell}

= Polarized membrane trafficking
{parent=Vesicle trafficking}

Preferential delivery, recycling or removal of membrane components in different cellular domains. Domain-specific traffic converts positional information into unequal membrane growth and composition.

= Cardiomyocyte
{parent=Biological cell}

A cardiac muscle cell. Embryonic differentiation and contractile activity are compatible with continued proliferation; adult cell-cycle re-entry does not necessarily entail completed division.

= Cardiomyocyte hypertrophy
{parent=Cardiomyocyte}

Enlargement of individual cardiac muscle cells, distinct from an increase in cardiomyocyte number through completed division.

= Cell competition
{parent=Biological cell}

Neighbour-dependent selection among cell populations in which relatively less-fit cells are lost or displaced. Homogeneous toxicity or slower autonomous growth alone does not establish competitive elimination.

= Transit-amplifying cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A proliferative descendant population that expands cell output before differentiation. The number of divisions and capacity for reversal depend on tissue and context.

= Cell-autonomous
{parent=Biological cell}

A cellular phenotype attributable to the affected cell's own genotype or state under the tested conditions. Mosaic experiments distinguish this from effects transmitted through neighbouring cells or the environment.

= Bacteriocyte
{parent=Biological cell}

A <bacteriocyte> is a specialized host cell containing bacterial symbionts. The intracellular environment and transmission route shape selection on both partners.

= Reticulate body
{parent=Biological cell}

A <reticulate body> is the growth-associated form in the chlamydial developmental cycle. <genome> replication alone does not establish successful production of infectious elementary bodies.

= Elementary body
{parent=Biological cell}

An <elementary body> is the infectious form in the chlamydial developmental cycle. Environmental persistence during transmission is distinct from productive extracellular replication.

= Chlamydial inclusion
{parent=Biological cell}

A <chlamydial inclusion> is the membrane-bound intracellular compartment supporting chlamydial development. Host trafficking interactions and nutrient access are regulated there.

= Lipid droplet
{parent=Biological cell}

A <lipid droplet> is a cellular lipid-storage structure. Delivery of an engineered fatty acid into storage can be limited independently of the pathway that synthesizes it.

= Membrane contact site
{parent=Biological cell}

A <membrane contact site> closely apposes distinct membranes without fusing them. Demonstrating apposition establishes architecture, while transfer or signaling requires separate functional evidence.

= Endoplasmic reticulum-mitochondrial contact site
{parent=Membrane contact site}

An <endoplasmic reticulum-mitochondrial contact site> brings ER and mitochondrial membranes close while preserving their separate compartments. Specific contacts can contribute to <calcium> handling, lipid exchange and organelle organization.

= Chinese hamster ovary cell
{c}
{parent=Biological cell}

A <Chinese hamster ovary cell> is a mammalian cell type widely used in recombinant-protein production. Product glycosylation, assembly and quality depend on the engineered line and culture process.

= HAT selection
{c}
{parent=Biological cell}

<HAT selection> uses hypoxanthine, aminopterin and thymidine to distinguish cells able to maintain nucleotide supply through salvage metabolism under de novo synthesis blockade. In a conventional hybridoma scheme it selects suitable fusion products, not antigen specificity.

= Myeloma
{parent=Biological cell}

A <myeloma> is a malignancy of the plasma-cell lineage. Selected cultured myeloma lines provide an immortal fusion partner in hybridoma production; their metabolic selection properties depend on the particular line.

= B lymphocyte
{c}
{parent=Biological cell}

A <B lymphocyte> belongs to the antibody-producing lineage of the adaptive immune system. Recovery of an antigen-binding clone does not alone establish a useful therapeutic antibody or an immortal production line.

= Tumour-infiltrating lymphocyte
{parent=Biological cell}

A lymphocyte recovered from within a <tumour> specimen. Presence in a specimen does not alone establish effective recognition or killing of malignant cells.

= Pancreatic stellate cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A stromal cell of the pancreas that can adopt activated states and contribute to <extracellular matrix> and metabolic interactions in pancreatic disease.

= T cell
{parent=Biological cell}
{c}

A lymphocyte using a T-cell receptor for antigen recognition. Distinct populations have cytotoxic, helper, regulatory and memory functions.

= CD4
{parent=T cell}
{c}

A coreceptor usually associated with recognition of peptide–MHC-class-II complexes. CD4 T-cell populations coordinate and regulate immune responses in addition to other context-dependent activities.

= CD28
{parent=T cell}
{c}

A T-cell costimulatory receptor. Its interaction with B7 ligands on activated antigen-presenting cells contributes to naive-cell priming.

= CD8
{parent=T cell}
{c}

A coreceptor associated with recognition of peptide–MHC-class-I complexes by cytotoxic T cells. It contributes to binding and signalling without replacing T-cell-receptor specificity.

= Dendritic cell
{parent=Biological cell}

An antigen-presenting immune cell that can help initiate T-cell responses. Antigen uptake alone does not establish effective activating presentation.

= Myeloid-derived suppressor cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A myeloid population defined partly by its ability to suppress immune responses, particularly in disease-associated settings such as cancer.

= Regulatory T cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A T-cell population with immune-suppressive functions that can restrain both pathological inflammation and antitumour responses.

= Endothelial cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A cell lining a blood or lymphatic vessel. Endothelial state influences barrier function, perfusion and interactions with circulating cells.

= Tumour-associated macrophage
{parent=Biological cell}

A macrophage within or associated with a <tumour microenvironment>. Its contribution to invasion, vascular remodelling or immunity depends on the particular functional state.

= Macrophage
{parent=Biological cell}

An innate immune cell that can phagocytose material and regulate tissue and immune responses. Its behaviour varies with local signals and cellular history.

= Cancer-associated fibroblast
{parent=Biological cell}

A fibroblast in a tumour-associated state. Some functions support <tumour> progression while other fibroblast populations or functions can restrain it; the label alone does not establish the causal effect.

= Fibroblast
{parent=Biological cell}

A connective-tissue cell with roles in <extracellular matrix> production and tissue organisation. Fibroblast populations and activation states are heterogeneous.

= Cell growth
{parent=Biological cell}

An increase in cellular biomass or size. It differs from <cell proliferation>, which increases the number of cells.

= Epithelial cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A cell participating in an organised epithelial layer, with context-dependent polarity, adhesion and barrier or transport functions.

= MDCK cell
{parent=Epithelial cell}
{c}

= MDCK
{synonym}
{c}

Madin-Darby canine kidney cell, used in three-dimensional <epithelial cyst> and polarity assays.

= Keratinocyte
{parent=Epithelial cell}

= Basal keratinocyte
{parent=Keratinocyte}

A <keratinocyte> in the basement-membrane-associated compartment of a <stratified epithelium>. Basal position does not establish that every such cell is actively dividing.

= Paneth cell
{parent=Epithelial cell}
{c}

A specialized secretory cell normally found at the base of mammalian small-intestinal crypts. Its local antimicrobial and niche-support roles do not imply that all intestinal stem-cell systems require a Paneth-cell arrangement.

= Tuft cell
{parent=Epithelial cell}

A specialized epithelial sensory cell population with characteristic apical morphology and context-dependent signalling roles.

= Enteroendocrine cell
{parent=Epithelial cell}

A specialized intestinal epithelial cell that senses local conditions and releases signalling products, including hormones.

= Goblet cell
{parent=Epithelial cell}

A specialized mucus-secreting epithelial cell, present in the intestinal lining and other mucosal tissues.

= Enterocyte
{parent=Epithelial cell}

An absorptive epithelial cell of the intestinal lining. Enterocyte-lineage progenitors and terminally differentiated villus enterocytes need not have the same regenerative potential.

= Biological cell division
{parent=Biological cell}

Partition of one cell into descendant cells. Copying and allocating inherited material must be coordinated with physical separation, but nuclear division and <cytokinesis> are distinct events.

= Binary fission
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Biological cell division}

= Binary fission
{synonym}

A <biological cell division> that produces two daughter cells. It is an <asexual reproduction> mechanism in many unicellular organisms and is distinct from the exchange or recombination of genetic material during sexual processes.

= Endomembrane system
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <endomembrane system> comprises functionally connected internal membranes such as the <endoplasmic reticulum>, <Golgi apparatus>, <endosomes> and <lysosomes>, together with their transport carriers. Connections through carrier traffic differ from direct continuity of the membrane sheets.

= Topology of membrane compartments
{parent=Endomembrane system}

Ordinary vesicle budding and fusion preserve which side of a membrane a protein domain faces: a cytoplasmic domain remains cytoplasmic, and a luminal domain remains luminal. Invagination can enclose previously external space without transporting its contents through a bilayer. The <endoplasmic reticulum> lumen is continuous with the space between the two <nuclear envelope> membranes; it is not the chromosome-containing nucleoplasm. Any proposed evolutionary remodelling must account for these distinct spaces and the membrane connections between them.

= Leukocyte
{parent=Biological cell}

= White blood cell
{synonym}

= Cell adhesion
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

<Cell adhesion> is attachment of a <biological cell> to another cell or to its surroundings through molecular interactions. Adhesion strength and turnover help determine how cells transmit forces and rearrange.

= Adherens junction
{parent=Cell adhesion}

A cell-cell junction coupling adhesion to the cytoskeleton. It can transmit mechanical load while being remodelled during epithelial movements.

= Focal adhesion
{parent=Cell adhesion}
{wiki}

A <focal adhesion> is a multiprotein connection between <integrins> and the <actin> <cytoskeleton>. It can transmit traction and change its assembly in response to loading.

= Cell type
{parent=Biological cell}

A class of <biological cells> distinguished by characteristic structure, function or molecular state. Comparing mixed <biological tissues> can confound changes within a <cell type> with changes in the abundance of that class.

= Subcellular localization
{parent=Biological cell}

<subcellular localization> is the distribution of a component among cellular regions or compartments. Changing localization can change a <protein>'s partners even when its total abundance is constant.

= Blastomere
{parent=Biological cell}

A cell produced by the early <cleavage divisions> of an embryo.

= Blastomere recombination experiment
{parent=Blastomere}

Isolated <blastomeres> are reapposed in chosen combinations to test induction. Reciprocal wild-type and <mutant> combinations can separate a signalling-<cell> defect from a responding-<cell> defect.

= P2 blastomere
{parent=Blastomere}
{c}

An early posterior <Caenorhabditis elegans> <blastomere> that contacts EMS and provides inductive information required for normal <endoderm> specification.

= MS blastomere
{parent=Blastomere}
{c}

The sister of the <E blastomere> in <Caenorhabditis elegans>. Its descendants include <muscle> and part of the pharynx; it is not the normal intestinal founder.

= E blastomere
{parent=Blastomere}
{c}

The founder of the <Caenorhabditis elegans> intestinal lineage. Following its descendants distinguishes failure of <endoderm> specification from a failure of <terminal differentiation>.

= EMS blastomere
{parent=Blastomere}
{c}

The early <Caenorhabditis elegans> <blastomere> that divides into MS and E. Its daughters normally produce predominantly mesodermal derivatives and the intestine, respectively.

= Cell polarity
{parent=Biological cell}

A spatial asymmetry of cellular organisation, including distinct apical and basal domains, that can influence division, signalling and lineage allocation.

= Polarized cell growth
{parent=Cell polarity}

Growth concentrated at a selected part of the <biological cell> surface. Spatial selection can be controlled at transport, <vesicle docking> or fusion rather than only by directed vesicle motion.

= Planar cell polarity
{parent=Cell polarity}

Coordinated asymmetry within the plane of a tissue, distinct from the apical-basal axis. Not every case of planar polarization uses the same molecular pathway.

= PAR polarity complex
{parent=Cell polarity}
{c}

A group of interacting polarity regulators including PAR-3, PAR-6 and atypical protein kinase C. Its organization links cortical domains to junctional and morphogenetic behaviour.

= Apical-basal polarity
{parent=Cell polarity}

Organization of epithelial cells into distinct apical and basolateral domains. Individual apical identity and coordination of apical surfaces into a shared tissue lumen are related but distinct properties.

= Scribble polarity complex
{parent=Apical-basal polarity}

Basolateral polarity regulators including <Scribble>, Discs large and Lethal giant larvae. Their interactions oppose inappropriate apical-domain expansion while supporting other tissue-specific functions.

= Crumbs polarity complex
{parent=Apical-basal polarity}

Apical-domain regulators organized around Crumbs-family proteins and associated scaffolds. Tissue-specific interactions and dosage influence apical membrane organization.

= Apoptosis
{parent=Biological cell}

A regulated form of cell death. Measuring it helps distinguish failed <cell specification> from selective loss of an already specified population.

= BAX
{parent=Apoptosis}
{c}

A BCL-2-family effector promoting mitochondrial outer-membrane permeabilization during apoptosis. Its activation differs from merely increasing expression of an apoptotic gene.

= NOXA
{parent=Apoptosis}
{c}

A proapoptotic BH3-only BCL-2-family protein that can be induced by p53. Its effects depend on the survival-protein context.

= PUMA
{parent=Apoptosis}
{c}

A proapoptotic BH3-only BCL-2-family protein induced in some p53 responses, contributing to mitochondrial apoptosis.

= Cell intercalation
{parent=Biological cell}

A rearrangement in which cells insert between neighbours, altering tissue shape and adjacency without requiring cell division.

= Mediolateral intercalation
{parent=Cell intercalation}

Rearrangement in which <cells> move between neighbours along the mediolateral axis, contributing to convergence and extension of a tissue.

= Radial intercalation
{parent=Cell intercalation}

Movement between <cells> in different layers, tending to thin and spread a tissue.

= Cell proliferation
{parent=Biological cell}

An increase in the number of <biological cells> through cell division. Cell number and tissue volume are distinct measurements because cell size and packing can change.

= Clonal lymphoproliferation
{parent=Cell proliferation}

Expansion of a lymphocyte lineage sharing clonal ancestry. Detection of a clone is not by itself proof of aggressive malignant disease.

= Contact inhibition
{parent=Cell proliferation}

Reduced proliferation or movement in response to cellular contacts under a specified context. Tissue growth control involves additional signals beyond contact alone.

= Cell migration
{parent=Biological cell}

<Cell migration> is movement of a <biological cell> relative to its surroundings. In crawling cells, contact forces with a substrate can propel the cell, while <chemotaxis> can bias its direction in response to a chemical <gradient>.

= Tissue advection
{parent=Cell migration}

Displacement of <cells> with bulk tissue motion. Subtracting the surrounding flow is needed to distinguish autonomous crawling from <passive transport>.

= Contact guidance
{parent=Cell migration}

<Contact guidance> is a directional influence of physical structures, such as aligned fibres or grooves, on <cell migration>. It can organize trajectories without an imposed soluble <concentration gradient>.

= Haptotaxis
{parent=Cell migration}
{wiki}

<Haptotaxis> is directed <cell migration> along spatial variation of an immobilized adhesive or guidance cue. It differs from guidance by a soluble <chemoattractant> field.

= Durotaxis
{parent=Cell migration}
{wiki}

<Durotaxis> is biased <cell migration> associated with spatial variation in substrate stiffness. Distinguish a directional bias from changes in speed, attachment or survival on different substrates.

= Chemokinesis
{parent=Cell migration}
{wiki}

<Chemokinesis> is a change in motility associated with a chemical stimulus without a directional bias toward or away from its source. Measure speed and persistence separately from directional <chemotaxis>.

= Membrane bleb
{parent=Cell migration}

A <membrane bleb> is a rounded protrusion formed when the <cell membrane> locally detaches from or loses support by the <cell cortex>. Pressure expands the initially cortex-poor region; cortical reassembly can then stabilize or retract it.

= Lamellipodium
{parent=Cell migration}
{wiki}

A <lamellipodium> is a broad, thin, <actin>-rich protrusion at a cell edge. <Actin> polymerization pushes the <cell membrane>, but net advance also depends on mechanical coupling to the environment.

= Collective cell migration
{parent=Cell migration}

In <collective cell migration>, neighbouring <biological cells> move with coordinated trajectories while retaining interactions that influence their motion. Mechanical coupling, contact-dependent signals and soluble cues can each contribute. A coherent group need not consist of identically behaving cells: leaders and followers may perform different roles.

= Velocity correlation in cell migration
{parent=Collective cell migration}

A quantitative measure of coordinated <cell> velocities. A common external force can create correlation without proving direct <cell>-to-<cell> transmission of guidance.

= Contact inhibition of locomotion
{parent=Collective cell migration}

<Contact inhibition of locomotion> is a contact-triggered change of protrusions and movement direction. It is distinct from contact inhibition of proliferation. In <neural crest> experiments, cell collisions caused protrusion collapse and repolarization; blocking noncanonical <Wnt signalling> disrupted the response.<Contact inhibition of locomotion in vivo controls neural crest directional migration by Carlos Carmona-Fontaine and others>[Collision and signalling-perturbation experiments]{ref}

= Cytoskeleton
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A network of protein filaments within a <biological cell> that contributes to shape, mechanical support and motion. Its coupling to the <lipid bilayer> adds mechanical responses, including shear elasticity, absent from an isolated fluid bilayer.

= Spectrin
{parent=Cytoskeleton}

= Actomyosin
{parent=Cytoskeleton}

An assembly of actin filaments and myosin motors. Network architecture, motor activity and attachments determine how contractile activity produces cellular or tissue deformation.

= Sarcomere
{parent=Cytoskeleton}

A repeating contractile unit of striated muscle containing organized filament systems. Its maturation contributes to force production but does not alone establish a cell's proliferative state.

= Actomyosin contractility
{parent=Cytoskeleton}

<actomyosin contractility> generates force through interactions of <myosin> motors with <actin> networks. The resulting load depends on network organization, attachments and resistance from the surroundings.

= Cell culture
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

<Cell culture> maintains <biological cells> outside their original <organism>.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Glossary, Cell culture]{ref}

= 3D cell culture
{parent=Cell culture}

= Three-dimensional cell culture
{synonym}

= Organoid
{parent=3D cell culture}
{wiki}

An <organoid> is a three-dimensional cellular model that reproduces selected organized features of a tissue or organ. Its relevance depends on which cell types, architecture and functions it actually preserves, rather than on three-dimensional shape alone.

= Intestinal organoid
{parent=Organoid}

A cultured three-dimensional intestinal epithelial structure reproducing selected stem-cell and differentiation behaviours. Exogenous growth factors and matrix replace important environmental inputs, so successful growth does not demonstrate signal-independent development.

= Tumour spheroid
{parent=3D cell culture}

A <tumour spheroid> is a three-dimensional aggregate of tumour cells used to study growth, transport and interactions with surrounding material. It need not reproduce the organized cellular diversity of an <organoid>.

= Primary cell culture
{parent=Cell culture}

A <primary cell culture> precedes the first successful <subculture>.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Glossary, Cell line and Primary culture]{ref}

= Cell culture medium
{parent=Cell culture}

A <cell culture medium> supplies a <cell culture>'s nutrients and chemical environment.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Culture media, Media formulations]{ref}

= Fetal bovine serum
{parent=Cell culture medium}

= Autoclave
{parent=Cell culture}

An <autoclave> uses heated, pressurized <water> vapor to sterilize suitable materials, including equipment for <cell culture>.

= Cell line
{parent=Biological cell}

A <cell line> comprises descendants after a <primary cell culture>'s first <subculture>. Further division can be finite or continuous; one founder <biological cell> is not required.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Glossary, Cell line, Clone and Continuous cell culture]{ref}

The useful continuity is ancestry across successive <subcultures>: descendants supply the next experiment. Individual <biological cells> can divide or die while their <cell line> persists. A common origin helps compare experiments, but does not imply that every descendant has identical <DNA>, <gene expression> or <cell state>.

= Caco-2
{parent=Cell line}
{c}

A human colorectal-carcinoma-derived epithelial cell line used in models including three-dimensional cyst organization. Its behaviour should not be assumed identical to that of a normal embryonic epithelium.

= Immortalised cell line
{parent=Cell line}
{wiki}

= Continuous cell line
{synonym}

= Immortalized cell line
{synonym}

An <immortalised cell line> can apparently sustain unlimited <cell line population doublings> under suitable conditions. Immortality describes lineage reproduction, not individual <biological cell> survival.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Glossary, Continuous cell culture and Immortalization]{ref}

= Subculture
{parent=Cell line}
{wiki=Subculture_(biology)}

A <subculture> transfers part of a <cell culture> into fresh <cell culture medium>; each transfer adds one passage.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Cell growth and propagation, Passage number and population doubling level]{ref}

= Cell culture passage accounting
{parent=Subculture}
{title2=$N_{\mathrm{seed}}=fN_{\mathrm{harvest}}$}

In an original constructed example, seed $300\,000$ <biological cells> in $6\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <cell culture medium>. Suppose the recoverable count becomes $1\,200\,000$ before the next <subculture>. Transfer a well-mixed quarter of that harvest into a new vessel and bring its final <volume (thermodynamics)> to $6\,\mathrm{mL}$. Assume complete recovery, no loss during transfer and no division during handling. The selected portion contains
$$
N_{\mathrm{seed}}=\frac14(1\,200\,000)=300\,000,\qquad c_{\mathrm{seed}}=\frac{300\,000}{6\,\mathrm{mL}}=50\,000\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.
$$
The other $900\,000$ <biological cells> remain outside the tracked vessel. The lower count after transfer is selection of a portion, not evidence of death. Adding fresh <cell culture medium> lowers the <number density> further without changing the selected count.

\Image[cell-culture-passage-and-doubling-accounting.png]
{title=One <subculture> transfer can follow two <cell line population doublings>}
{description=Original constructed count ledger, not an observed <HeLa> growth curve. Each growth interval multiplies the recoverable count by four; each transfer retains one quarter. The tracked lineage gains four net <cell line population doublings> across two intervals although both seed counts are $300\,000$. Boxes describe whole cultured populations, not persistent individual <biological cells>.}

If the next interval again changes $300\,000$ to $1\,200\,000$, the two growth intervals contribute $2+2=4$ net <cell line population doublings>. At the second harvest there has been just one intervening passage. Another quarter-transfer would add another passage without adding a growth interval. Thus neither passage number nor the <logarithm> of the final count divided by the very first seed count records all growth when portions are removed.

= Cell line population doublings
{parent=Cell line}
{title2=$d=\log_2(N_1/N_0)$}

For positive recoverable counts $N_0,N_1$ bracketing one growth interval, define its net <cell line population doublings> by
$$
d=\log_2\!\left(\frac{N_1}{N_0}\right)=\frac{\ln(N_1/N_0)}{\ln2}.
$$
The formula counts how many factors of two produce the observed net factor. For exactly two divisions per original <biological cell>, with all daughters recovered, $N_1=2^2N_0$, giving $d=2$. Death or loss of <biological cells> makes this a net estimate of division.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Passage number and population doubling level, PDL equation and loss caveat]{ref}

Under constant-rate <exponential growth>, $N(t)=N_0e^{rt}$, a growth interval of duration $\Delta t$ gives
$$
r=\frac{\ln(N_1/N_0)}{\Delta t},\qquad t_2=\frac{\ln2}{r}=\frac{\Delta t}{d}.
$$
For the constructed fourfold increase over $48\,\mathrm{h}$, $d=2$ and the model <doubling time> is $24\,\mathrm{h}$. The proof follows by taking the <natural logarithm> and solving $e^{rt_2}=2$. This is a fitted property of that interval and model: it is not a universal <HeLa> <doubling time>, nor the interval between two divisions of each individual <biological cell>. Across successive growth intervals, add their $d$ values; keep transfer losses separate as in <cell culture passage accounting>.

= Cell counting
{parent=Cell line}

<Cell counting> estimates the number of <biological cells> in a preparation. A count per calibrated <volume (thermodynamics)> gives <number density>; multiplying by the whole preparation's <volume (thermodynamics)> estimates its total count. A <trypan blue exclusion test> adds an operational distinction between dye-excluding and dye-stained <biological cells>.

= Hemocytometer
{parent=Cell counting}
{wiki}

A <hemocytometer> has a calibrated grid and depth. ATCC describes $1\,\mathrm{mm}^2$ squares $0.1\,\mathrm{mm}$ deep, each sampling $10^{-4}\,\mathrm{mL}$.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Cell counting, chamber geometry; Figure 2]{ref}

An original constructed <hemocytometer> count uses four such squares after mixing the <biological cell> preparation with the same <volume (thermodynamics)> of stain. The dye-excluding counts are $36,44,40,40$; the dye-stained counts are $4,6,5,5$. The dilution factor is $D=2$. With $v$ the sampled <volume (thermodynamics)> per square and $\bar n$ the <arithmetic mean> count per square,
$$
v=(1\,\mathrm{mm}^2)(0.1\,\mathrm{mm})=10^{-4}\,\mathrm{mL},\qquad c=D\frac{\bar n}{v}.
$$
Hence the undiluted preparation has dye-excluding <number density> $2(40)/10^{-4}=800\,000\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and total <number density> $2(45)/10^{-4}=900\,000\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$. Its dye-excluding fraction is $160/180=8/9\approx88.9\%$. If the well-mixed preparation contains $3\,\mathrm{mL}$, it contains an estimated $2\,400\,000$ dye-excluding <biological cells>. The chamber sample itself contains only $160$ counted dye-excluding <biological cells>.

\Image[hemocytometer-count-volume-and-dilution.png]
{title=A sampled count becomes a whole-preparation estimate through <volume (thermodynamics)> and dilution}
{description=Original constructed <hemocytometer> example: four $1\,\mathrm{mm}^2$ counting squares at $0.1\,\mathrm{mm}$ depth. Numbers report hypothetical counts, not recorded <HeLa> measurements. Correcting for the equal-volume stain mixture doubles both <number densities> and leaves the dye-excluding fraction unchanged.}

The relation $c=D\bar n/v$ follows from counting material in a known <volume (thermodynamics)>, then undoing dilution. It assumes a representative mixed sample and correctly identified single <biological cells>. Clumps, inconsistent decisions at grid edges, wrong chamber depth and nonrepresentative sampling introduce <measurement error>. A fixed convention such as including top and left edges while excluding bottom and right edges assigns shared-edge centers to just one square. Adding ten times as much suspension to a correctly filled chamber does not make each square's calibrated <volume (thermodynamics)> ten times larger.

= Trypan blue exclusion test
{parent=Cell counting}

The <trypan blue exclusion test> reads <cell membrane> integrity through <trypan blue> exclusion. Prolonged dye exposure can itself increase staining.<Animal Cell Culture Guide by ATCC>[Cell viability, dye exclusion and trypan-blue counting window]{ref}

An unstained <biological cell> is dye-excluding at the time of this assay. That observation alone does not demonstrate future division, sustained <metabolism> or long-term renewal of a <cell line>. Conversely, a smaller unstained count can reflect both staining and loss of <biological cells> from the recovered preparation. Report the denominator and recovery procedure alongside the fraction.

= HeLa
{parent=Cell line}
{c}
{wiki}

<HeLa> is a human <continuous cell line> originating in <cervical cancer> <biological cells> collected from <Henrietta Lacks> in 1951. Its name combines the beginning of her first and last names.<The Legacy of Henrietta Lacks (Johns Hopkins Medicine)>[The Legacy of Henrietta Lacks, opening history and name explanation]{ref}

ATCC's CCL-2 <HeLa> preparation grows attached to a surface. Its product sheet specifies a <cell culture medium> supplemented with <fetal bovine serum>, at $37\,{}^\circ\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%$ <carbon dioxide> in air.<HeLa - CCL-2 by ATCC>[Characteristics, Growth properties; Handling information, Complete medium, Temperature and Atmosphere]{ref} These are specified conditions for that preparation, rather than defining conditions for every <HeLa> derivative.

Continuing access to related <biological cells> makes <HeLa> useful for comparisons of <cell state> and responses to interventions. But a <continuous cell line> is a laboratory model: its history and environment matter, and observations in it do not alone establish responses in an intact <organism>. <Cell counting> measures abundance; a <trypan blue exclusion test> measures a particular membrane-associated readout; neither proves <cell line> identity.

= HeLa culture viability experiment
{parent=HeLa}
{c}

A published teaching experiment used ATCC CCL-2 <HeLa> to make the distinction between a <cell line> and the condition of individual <biological cells> visible. Cultures were expanded in treated flasks and supplied as adherent dishes. A wash with <phosphate-buffered saline> preceded <trypsin> treatment to detach them; added <fetal bovine serum> stopped the digestion. The dispersed preparation was mixed with an equal <volume (thermodynamics)> of <trypan blue> and loaded into a counting chamber under an <optical microscope>.<Introducing Mammalian Cell Culture and Cell Viability Techniques in the Undergraduate Biology Laboratory by Kristen Bowey-Dellinger and others>[Procedure, Materials; HeLa cell washing and dissociation; HeLa cell counting]{ref}

For the <ethanol> comparison, treatment groups and an untreated control were brought to the same final <volume (thermodynamics)>; the control received <phosphate-buffered saline>. Dye-excluding counts were compared across <replicates>. The paper reports representative student measurements in Figure 3A and documents the untreated control in its discussion. Its alternative <temperature> comparison used three <replicates> per condition and a $30\,\mathrm{min}$ exposure.<Introducing Mammalian Cell Culture and Cell Viability Techniques in the Undergraduate Biology Laboratory by Kristen Bowey-Dellinger and others>[HeLa cell viability—biology majors, volume-matched control; Figure 3A; Evidence of student learning—biology majors; HeLa cell viability—general biology education]{ref}

The paper's disposable chamber had a different count-to-<number density> calibration from the <hemocytometer> above. Dye exclusion does not establish future division; recovery and mixing also affect counts. The constructed <hemocytometer> example is independent of those observations.<Introducing Mammalian Cell Culture and Cell Viability Techniques in the Undergraduate Biology Laboratory by Kristen Bowey-Dellinger and others>[Sample data—general biology education, Glasstic Slide 10 calibration; Faculty instructions, chamber equations can differ]{ref}

= Cell state
{parent=Biological cell}

A <cell state> describes current properties of a <biological cell>, including its <gene expression>, structure and activity. A snapshot of <cell state> does not alone establish its future <cell fate>.

= Stem cell
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <stem cell> can produce both renewed <stem cells> and differentiated descendants. Its developmental range depends on the type and setting; self-renewal does not imply unrestricted <cell fate>.

= Pluripotency
{parent=Stem cell}

Capacity to generate the principal embryonic <cell lineages> under suitable conditions. Expression of one stem-<cell> marker alone does not establish this capacity.

= Primed pluripotency
{parent=Pluripotency}

A pluripotent state associated with later <epiblast> and readiness to respond to lineage-specifying signals. It is experimentally and molecularly distinct from <naive pluripotency>.

= Formative pluripotency
{parent=Pluripotency}

A transitional pluripotent state in which naive circuitry has been dismantled while competence for later lineage induction is being acquired. This is a state transition, not an additional <germ layer>.

= Naive pluripotency
{parent=Pluripotency}

A pluripotent state associated with the preimplantation mouse <epiblast> and a characteristic regulatory network. Naive <cells> differ from the postimplantation <epiblast> in signalling requirements, <chromatin> state and responsiveness to differentiation cues.

= Population self-renewal
{parent=Stem cell}

Maintenance of a renewing population through balanced outcomes across cells, without requiring every division to produce exactly one renewing daughter.

= Single-progenitor model
{parent=Population self-renewal}

A model in which one functionally defined progenitor class can explain renewal through stochastic, balanced fates. Molecular uniformity and exact independence of neighbouring cells are stronger claims than the model requires.

= Stem-cell ablation
{parent=Stem cell}

Experimental removal of a population defined by location or marker. The result depends on efficiency, duration, surviving cells, niche injury and the ability of other cells to replace the targeted state.

= Diphtheria toxin receptor ablation
{parent=Stem-cell ablation}

Ablation of engineered receptor-expressing cells using diphtheria toxin. Receptor-negative toxin controls, time-course verification and survival of untargeted compartments are essential.

= Germline stem cell
{parent=Stem cell}

A self-renewing cell that supplies descendants to the germline. Division orientation relative to a <stem cell niche> can help give daughters different developmental outcomes.

= Spectrosome
{parent=Germline stem cell}

A specialized cytoplasmic structure in relevant fly germline cells associated with localization of cell-cycle regulators. Its organization must not be assumed identical in every stem-cell system.

= Stem-cell niche
{parent=Stem cell}

The local cellular and extracellular environment regulating stem-cell persistence, renewal and differentiation. In culture, supplied matrix and growth factors may substitute for components normally provided by neighbouring cells.

= Multipotency
{parent=Stem cell}

Capacity to generate several specified differentiated cell types. It does not imply ability to generate every cell type or a whole organism.

= Self-renewal
{parent=Stem cell}

Production of descendants that retain the originating population's developmental capacity. For a stem-cell population, self-renewal must accompany suitable differentiated output; persistence of a label alone does not establish both properties.

= Intestinal stem cell
{parent=Stem cell}

A <stem cell> contributing sustained differentiated descendants to the intestinal lining. Marker expression is suggestive; long-term <lineage tracing> tests renewal and lineage output.

= Reserve intestinal stem cell
{parent=Intestinal stem cell}

A functional description for a cell that can restore intestinal stem-cell output after damage. It does not by itself establish a permanently distinct, uniquely marked quiescent lineage.

= Crypt-base columnar cell
{parent=Intestinal stem cell}

A cell at the crypt base, including actively cycling Lgr5-expressing <stem cells> interspersed with supporting cells in the mouse small intestine.

= Embryonic stem cell
{parent=Stem cell}

A <stem cell> derived from early embryonic tissue with capacity to generate many differentiated <cell types> under suitable conditions.

= Progenitor cell
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <progenitor cell> is a precursor with relatively restricted differentiation potential and, generally, more limited self-renewal than a <stem cell>. Usage depends on the lineage and assay; an expression marker alone does not establish renewal capacity.

= Primordial germ cell-like cell
{parent=Progenitor cell}

An experimentally induced cell resembling an embryonic <primordial germ cell> by molecular and developmental criteria. Similarity should be stated separately from demonstrated gamete-forming function.

= Pole cell
{parent=Progenitor cell}

One of the early germline precursor cells formed at the embryonic poles in <Drosophila melanogaster>, normally at the posterior where <germ plasm> is assembled.

= Primordial germ cell
{parent=Progenitor cell}

An early embryonic <germline> precursor that will contribute to future <gametes>. Expression of one germ-cell-associated marker alone does not demonstrate full developmental function.

= Neuromesodermal progenitor
{parent=Progenitor cell}

A <progenitor cell> with competence for neural and mesodermal development, particularly <spinal cord> and <paraxial mesoderm>. Shared markers indicate <cell state>; demonstrating realised bipotency requires <clonal lineage tracing>.

= Secretory granule
{parent=Biological cell}

A storage compartment that participates in regulated <exocytosis>; the <insulin> example distinguishes storage of a <hormone> from storage of <glycogen> fuel.

\Include[cell-cycle]{parent=Biological cell}

= Cell envelope
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cell envelope> comprises a <biological cell>'s surrounding boundary layers, including its <cell membrane> and a <cell wall> or additional outer structures when present. The term is especially useful when discussing <bacteria>, where the layers must be distinguished.

= Bacterial outer membrane
{parent=Cell envelope}
{wiki}

A <bacterial outer membrane> is an additional membrane outside the <peptidoglycan> in typical <Gram-negative bacteria>. It adds a barrier while the inner <cell membrane> remains the boundary of the <cytoplasm>.

= Periplasm
{parent=Bacterial outer membrane}

The <periplasm> is the compartment between the cytoplasmic and outer membranes in a typical Gram-negative bacterial envelope. Its components and the outer membrane can gate access of external electron acceptors.

= Cell wall
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <cell wall> is a supporting structure outside a <cell membrane>. It helps preserve shape and bears the load when <osmosis> raises internal <pressure>. The <cell membrane> supplies the <selective permeability> described in its own article; the <cell wall> supplies mechanical reinforcement. These functions cooperate: a porous supporting layer can restrain expansion without being the boundary that retains dissolved <solutes>. <Turgor pressure> makes this cooperation visible in a firm leaf; <plasmolysis> and a <lysozyme wall-removal experiment> separate the two functions experimentally.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/5.2 Passive Transport>[Biology 2e, §5.2, Tonicity in Living Systems, Figure 5.13: wall support and plasmolysis]{ref}

The material is not universal. Most <bacteria> use <peptidoglycan>, whose linked <sugar> chains and <amino acid> cross-links are explained below. A <plant cell wall> uses <cellulose>; a <fungal cell wall> contains <chitin> and <glucans>. <Animals> lack a <cell wall>, some <bacteria> are naturally wall-free, and <archaea> have different wall chemistry. Thus a <cell wall> is neither a universal cellular component nor one chemical recipe.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/22.2 Structure of Prokaryotes: Bacteria and Archaea>[Biology 2e, §22.2, Figure 22.13 and The Cell Wall of Prokaryotes: wall-free bacteria and archaeal differences]{ref}<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/4.3 Eukaryotic Cells>[Biology 2e, §4.3, Animal Cells versus Plant Cells and The Cell Wall: cellulose and animal comparison]{ref}<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/24.1 Characteristics of Fungi>[Biology 2e, §24.1, Cell Structure and Function: chitin and glucans]{ref}

\Image[cell-envelope-comparison.png]
{title=Representative <cell envelopes>: different supporting layers outside the <cell membrane>}
{description=Original layer-order schematic comparing <Gram-positive bacteria>, <Gram-negative bacteria>, a <plant cell wall>, a <fungal cell wall>, a member of <archaea> with a protein wall and an <animal> cell. Colors distinguish a <cell wall> from a <cell membrane> and a <bacterial outer membrane>. Thicknesses and spacing are illustrative, not measurements. These examples do not exhaust variation within any group.}

The <bacterial outer membrane> in typical <Gram-negative bacteria> is an additional membrane outside the <peptidoglycan>, not another name for that supporting mesh. The <cell envelope> includes the boundary layers together. An extra barrier can change access to the <peptidoglycan>, so response to <lysozyme> cannot be inferred from the mere presence of a <cell wall>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/22.2 Structure of Prokaryotes: Bacteria and Archaea>[Biology 2e, §22.2, Figure 22.16: inner membrane, peptidoglycan and outer membrane]{ref}

= Cell-wall pulse labelling
{parent=Cell wall}

Labelling of existing accessible wall followed by growth without the same free label. Comparing old label with new surface distinguishes inheritance of old wall from local deposition of new material.

= Plant cell wall
{parent=Cell wall}

A <plant cell wall> provides reinforcement outside the <cell membrane>, with <cellulose> as a major structural component. It is a composite rather than a solid sheet of pure <cellulose>. Wall construction varies with the kind and developmental state of the <biological cell>.

= Fungal cell wall
{parent=Cell wall}

A <fungal cell wall> contains structural <polysaccharides> including <chitin> and <glucans>. Its composition and organization vary among <fungi>; the diagram illustrates a composite rather than a pure <chitin> shell. A <fungus> sharing a <cell wall> with a <plant> does not imply shared wall chemistry.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/24.1 Characteristics of Fungi>[Biology 2e, §24.1, Cell Structure and Function: structural wall polysaccharides]{ref}

= Chitin
{parent=Fungal cell wall}
{tag=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

<Chitin> is a structural <polysaccharide> built from modified <sugar> units. It contributes reinforcement to a <fungal cell wall>.

= Glucan
{parent=Fungal cell wall}
{tag=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

A <glucan> is a <polysaccharide> built from <glucose> units. Different linkages produce different structures; some <glucans> contribute to a <fungal cell wall>.

= Turgor pressure
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}
{title2=$P_{\mathrm{in}}-P_{\mathrm{out}}$}

<Turgor pressure> is the internal <hydrostatic pressure> above the external <pressure> in a <biological cell>. When <water> enters a walled <biological cell> by <osmosis>, expansion loads the <cell wall>. Rising <pressure> can oppose further net entry while the <cell membrane> retains its <selective permeability>. Mechanical support therefore does not require the <cell wall> to block <water>.

As an original mechanical check, idealize a small flat patch of <cell wall> of <surface area> $A$ under uniform <turgor pressure> $\Delta P=P_{\mathrm{in}}-P_{\mathrm{out}}$. Its outward <force> is $F=\Delta P A$. Doubling $A$ at fixed <pressure> doubles the load; equal internal and external <pressures> give zero net pressure load. This is a force balance, not a measured wall strength or a complete model of a curved, growing <biological cell>. It says nothing about which <solutes> cross the <cell membrane>.

= Plasmolysis
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

<Plasmolysis> is withdrawal of the membrane-bounded contents from parts of a <cell wall> after <water> loss. A sufficiently concentrated external <hypertonic solution>, with <solutes> effectively excluded by the <cell membrane> over the observation time, can cause this. The <cell wall> remains; the enclosed material contracts. <Plasmolysis> is therefore distinct from <osmotic lysis>, which breaks the cellular boundary during excessive swelling. A return to a less concentrated surrounding <chemical solution> can reverse <plasmolysis> if the <biological cell> remains functional.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/5.2 Passive Transport>[Biology 2e, §5.2, Tonicity in Living Systems and Figure 5.13: membrane withdrawal under hypertonic conditions]{ref}

\Image[cell-wall-osmotic-experiment-logic.png]
{title=<Plasmolysis> with a retained <cell wall>, and osmotic challenge after wall removal}
{description=Original qualitative experiment logic. The upper pair compares a turgid <plant> cell with <plasmolysis> in an external <hypertonic solution>. The lower pair compares a supported <protoplast> with <osmotic lysis> after dilution for a susceptible preparation. Arrows indicate interventions or possible net <water> movement, not measured rates. The dilution comparison concerns <lysozyme>-susceptible <bacteria>; the upper comparison concerns <plant> cells. No measured dimensions, times or survival fractions are implied.}

= Protoplast
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

A <protoplast> is the living contents of a <biological cell> bounded by its <cell membrane> after removal of the <cell wall>. Removing the <cell wall> need not immediately destroy the <cell membrane>; stability depends on the preparation and surroundings. <Lysozyme> acts on susceptible bacterial <peptidoglycan>, not on <cellulose> or <chitin>, so it is not a universal wall-removal <enzyme>.

= Lysozyme wall-removal experiment
{parent=Protoplast}

The 1953 paper <The isolation of protoplasts from Bacillus megaterium by controlled treatment with lysozyme by Claes Weibull> (pp. 688–690, Figures 2–3) tested <Bacillus megaterium> strain KM. Apparatus included a Klett-Summerson colorimeter with a red filter and a Spencer no. 8 <phase-contrast microscope>. <Lysozyme> in phosphate buffer produced empty residual bodies; adding $0.2\,\mathrm{M}$ <sucrose> preserved granular spheres. Diluting their surroundings caused rapid <osmotic lysis>.

The same study compared untreated, boiled, <lysozyme>-exposed and <sucrose>-protected samples. Filled, stoppered tubes reduced damage from air exposure. After <centrifugation>, a Beckman DU spectrophotometer measured ultraviolet <wave absorption> of the separated liquid. Protected samples resembled untreated controls in releasing little ultraviolet-absorbing material; boiled and unprotected samples released much more. Later-growth cultures reduced spontaneous breakdown as a confounder.<The isolation of protoplasts from Bacillus megaterium by controlled treatment with lysozyme by Claes Weibull>[pp. 688–689, Materials and Methods; pp. 692–694, release comparison and Figure 6]{ref}

Together, the shape and release readouts support removal of mechanical protection while a permeability barrier persists. They do not establish unchanged <selective permeability> to every <solute>, long-term viability, or universal susceptibility.

An instructive extension of this experimental logic is a matched comparison: <lysozyme> present or absent, crossed with osmotic support present or absent. Keep starting material, buffer, observation interval and handling comparable; include a blank to distinguish optical effects of reagents from changes in the <biological cells>. Then dilute already formed <protoplasts> as a separate intervention. This separates access and wall removal from failure after removal. These are proposed controls for interpreting the mechanism, not additional reported results from the historical paper. A fall in optical signal alone cannot establish rupture: shape changes also alter <scattering of light>.

= Peptidoglycan
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

<Peptidoglycan> is a mesh of linked <sugar> chains cross-linked by short chains of <amino acids> in the <cell walls> of most <bacteria>. Its absence from the <cell walls> of <archaea> helps distinguish envelope chemistry without confusing either <cell wall> with a <cell membrane>. See https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells[OpenStax Microbiology, 3.3: cell walls and peptidoglycan].

\Include[cell-membrane]{parent=Biological cell}

= Rod complex
{parent=Peptidoglycan}

A bacterial cell-envelope assembly involved in oriented lateral peptidoglycan synthesis in many rod-shaped species.

= Cytoplasm
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cytoplasm> is the material inside a <cell membrane> but outside the <cell nucleus>, when one is present. It includes the <cytosol>, the <organelles> suspended in it, and other structures; it is not just a name for the liquid. In a <prokaryote>, which has no <cell nucleus>, the <nucleoid> and <ribosomes> are within the <cytoplasm>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: cytoplasm and cytosol] and https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.2: prokaryotic organization].

\Image[eukaryotic-cell-compartments.png]
{title=<Cell nucleus>, <cytosol> and <organelles> in a nucleated <eukaryotic> <biological cell>}
{description=Original schematic: the pale blue region represents <cytosol>, while the surrounding <cytoplasm> also includes the depicted <mitochondrion> and <ribosomes>. The <cell nucleus> and <cell nucleolus> are excluded from the <cytoplasm>. A <nuclear pore complex> provides a route across the double <nuclear envelope>. Dimensions and component counts are illustrative; many structures are omitted.}

Try classifying four positions in the diagram. A point in the pale blue region belongs to both <cytosol> and <cytoplasm>. A point inside the <mitochondrion> belongs to an <organelle> included in the <cytoplasm>, but not to the surrounding <cytosol>. A point on a free <ribosome> belongs to the <cytoplasm>, but the <ribosome> itself is not <cytosol>. A point inside the <cell nucleus> belongs to neither <cytoplasm> nor <cytosol>. These distinctions prevent the misleading inference that everything outside the <cell nucleus> shares one undivided liquid environment.

The usefulness of the <cytoplasm> is this combination of shared surroundings and specialized structures: <enzymes> in the <cytosol> carry out many steps of <metabolism>, while <organelles> maintain their own internal conditions. A soluble <protein> made by a free <ribosome> may work in the <cytosol> or be transported elsewhere, including into the <cell nucleus>. Cellular organization therefore combines separation with exchange, rather than assigning each <protein> permanently to its place of synthesis. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell (2002): traffic between nucleus and cytosol].

= Germ plasm
{parent=Cytoplasm}

Specialised cytoplasmic material containing components that promote <germline> development when inherited by selected embryonic cells.

= Cytosol
{parent=Cytoplasm}
{wiki}

The <cytosol> is the aqueous part of the <cytoplasm> surrounding its structures, including <organelles>. It contains <water>, <ions> and dissolved <molecules>, including many <proteins>; it is a crowded working environment rather than pure <water>. The liquid inside a <cell nucleus> or a membrane-bounded <organelle> is not the surrounding <cytosol>.

= Nucleoid
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <nucleoid> is the region occupied by <chromosome> <DNA> in a <prokaryotic> <biological cell>. It has no surrounding <nuclear envelope>, but its <DNA> is organized rather than freely spread at random. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.2: the nucleoid].

= Organelle
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

An <organelle> is a specialized subcellular structure. Usage varies over whether non-membrane-bounded structures such as <ribosomes> count as <organelles>, so explicitly say membrane-bounded <organelles> when comparing <eukaryotic> compartmentalization with <prokaryotic> organization. <Mitochondria> and <chloroplasts> are membrane-bounded examples. See https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells[OpenStax Microbiology, 3.4: cellular compartments].

= Peroxisome
{parent=Organelle}

A membrane-bounded organelle with pathways including oxidative metabolism. Many matrix proteins are imported after cytosolic synthesis, including folded substrates.

= Organelle identity
{parent=Organelle}

The maintained combination of membrane composition, resident proteins, lumenal conditions, geometry and trafficking functions that distinguishes a compartment. Its molecules can turn over while this functional organization persists.

= Centrosome
{parent=Organelle}

A microtubule-organizing structure in relevant animal cells. Positioning centrosomes relative to a niche can prepare the orientation of a later mitotic spindle. Budding yeast instead has a <spindle pole body>.

= Mitosome
{parent=Organelle}

A highly reduced mitochondrion-related <organelle> retaining selected metabolic functions rather than oxidative <ATP> production. Functions differ among lineages, including <iron-sulfur cluster> maturation in <Giardia> and <sulfate activation> in <Entamoeba histolytica>.<Mitochondrial remnant organelles of Giardia function in iron-sulphur protein maturation by Jorge Tovar and others>[Abstract]{ref}

= Hydrogenosome
{parent=Organelle}

A mitochondrion-related <organelle> class associated with anaerobic metabolism and, in typical examples, <hydrogen> and <ATP> production. <Substrate-level phosphorylation> can supply energy without a conventional oxygen-terminated respiratory chain.<Hydrogenosome, a cytoplasmic organelle of the anaerobic flagellate Tritrichomonas foetus, and its role in pyruvate metabolism by Donald G. Lindmark and Miklós Müller>[Abstract]{ref}

= Endoplasmic reticulum
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

= ER
{c}
{synonym}

The <endoplasmic reticulum> is a membrane-enclosed network of tubules and flattened sacs in a typical <eukaryotic> <biological cell>. Its continuous boundary encloses the <ER lumen>, separate from the <cytosol>, and connects with the outer <nuclear envelope>. <Rough endoplasmic reticulum> and <smooth endoplasmic reticulum> are regions of this network.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/The Endoplasmic Reticulum>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; The Endoplasmic Reticulum, opening paragraph and Figure 9.1: continuous membrane and nuclear-envelope connection]{ref}

\Image[endoplasmic-reticulum-rough-smooth-network.png]
{title=One <endoplasmic reticulum> network with rough and smooth regions}
{description=Original conceptual cross-section: blue interiors connect, while attached <ribosomes> distinguish rough regions. Shapes and dimensions are illustrative, not measured fluorescence or <electron microscopy>.}

= Endoplasmic reticulum stress
{parent=Endoplasmic reticulum}

<endoplasmic reticulum stress> arises when ER demands exceed its processing or homeostatic capacity. It can alter folding, degradation and transport, confounding interpretation of a supposedly selective secretion defect.

= Endoplasmic reticulum lumen
{parent=Endoplasmic reticulum}

The <endoplasmic reticulum lumen> is the aqueous space enclosed by the ER membrane. A soluble lumenal cargo cannot directly contact a cytoplasmic coat across an intact membrane.

= Endoplasmic reticulum exit site
{parent=Endoplasmic reticulum}

An <endoplasmic reticulum exit site> is a region organizing export machinery and cargo departure from the ER. Local coat assembly, cargo concentration and membrane supply can be coordinated there.

= ER lumen
{c}
{parent=Endoplasmic reticulum}

The <ER lumen> is the internal aqueous compartment of the <endoplasmic reticulum>. A <ribosome> attached to its membrane sits on the <cytosol> side, not inside the <ER lumen>. This distinction separates the machinery making a <protein> from the compartment receiving it.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/The Endoplasmic Reticulum>[Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others; Membrane-bound Ribosomes Define the Rough ER, Figure 12-36: cytosolic ribosomes and lumenal delivery]{ref}

= Rough endoplasmic reticulum
{parent=Endoplasmic reticulum}

= RER
{c}
{synonym}

<Rough endoplasmic reticulum> bears attached <ribosomes> on its <cytosol> surface. In a common mammalian route, <protein targeting> delivers a <nascent chain> into the <ER lumen> during <biological translation>. Other <proteins> enter the membrane. Free and attached <ribosomes> share the same basic machinery; targeting depends on their cargo.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/The Endoplasmic Reticulum>[Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others; Membrane-bound Ribosomes Define the Rough ER, Figures 12-36 and 12-37; Translocation Across the ER Membrane Does Not Always Require Ongoing Polypeptide Chain Elongation]{ref}

= ER signal sequence
{c}
{parent=Rough endoplasmic reticulum}

An <ER signal sequence> is <polypeptide chain> targeting information recognized by machinery delivering a <protein> to the <endoplasmic reticulum>. Many soluble cargoes have a removable <N-terminus> signal; membrane insertion can use internal signals. An <ER signal sequence> alone does not specify the final destination.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/The Endoplasmic Reticulum>[Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others; The ER Signal Sequence Is Removed from Most Soluble Proteins After Translocation; In Single-Pass Transmembrane Proteins, a Single Internal ER Signal Sequence Remains in the Lipid Bilayer as a Membrane-spanning α Helix]{ref}

For an original counting example, stipulate an unbranched precursor <polypeptide chain> of $180$ <amino acid residues> whose first $20$ <amino acid residues> form a cleaved <ER signal sequence>. With no further processing, the remaining chain has $180-20=160$ <amino acid residues> and $159$ <peptide bonds>. The initial chain had $179$ <peptide bonds>; the detached signal has $19$, so $159+19=178$ remain after one bond is cut. The check counts adjacent positions: an unbranched chain of $n$ <amino acid residues> has $n-1$ <peptide bonds>. These stipulated lengths are not a natural sequence, a measured size or evidence that a particular sequence functions as an <ER signal sequence>.

= Smooth endoplasmic reticulum
{parent=Endoplasmic reticulum}

= SER
{c}
{synonym}

<Smooth endoplasmic reticulum> lacks attached <ribosomes>. Specialized regions support <lipid> <metabolism> and processing of foreign compounds; the <endoplasmic reticulum> also stores and releases <calcium> <ions>. Regional specialization does not split the network into two separate <organelles>, nor make every smooth region perform every function.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/The Endoplasmic Reticulum>[Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others; Smooth ER Is Abundant in Some Specialized Cells, Figures 12-38A and 12-38B and calcium-storage paragraphs]{ref}

= Secretory protein routing
{parent=Endoplasmic reticulum}

<Secretory protein routing> connects entry into the <endoplasmic reticulum>, <protein folding> and sorting, transfer to the <Golgi apparatus>, and delivery onward. In the conventional soluble-cargo route, <transport vesicles> carry lumenal contents between compartments; <exocytosis> releases cargo outside the <cell membrane>. Resident <endoplasmic reticulum> <proteins> can be retained or retrieved, and defective cargo can be held back. Many <cytosol> <proteins> never enter this route.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/The Endoplasmic Reticulum>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Protein Folding and Processing in the ER, Figure 9.14; Export of Proteins and Lipids from the ER, Figures 9.20 and 9.21]{ref}

\Image[secretory-protein-routing-and-membrane-sides.png]
{title=<Secretory protein routing> preserves membrane sides}
{description=Original conceptual route for soluble cargo and a <membrane protein>. Intermediate compartments and return routes are omitted. Arrows show connectivity, not measured timing, fluorescence or <electron microscopy>.}

The useful geometric rule is preservation of membrane sides: during budding and fusion, a lumen-facing region of a <membrane protein> stays lumen-facing, then faces the exterior after <exocytosis>; its <cytosol>-facing region stays cytosolic.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/The Endoplasmic Reticulum>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Insertion of Proteins into the ER Membrane, Figure 9.10; Export of Proteins and Lipids from the ER: preservation of orientation]{ref}

Work through the schematic using two distinguishable labels A and B on a stipulated <membrane protein>. Start with A facing the <ER lumen> and B facing the <cytosol>. At each budding or fusion step, carry the surrounding membrane together with the labels; do not turn the <protein> over. The resulting pair is A facing the <Golgi apparatus> interior and B facing the <cytosol>, then A facing a <transport vesicle> interior and B facing the <cytosol>, and finally A facing the exterior and B facing the <cytosol>. This is a stepwise argument about the depicted operations, not a claim that the compartments have identical chemical contents. Likewise, once soluble cargo is inside the <ER lumen>, this schematic reaches the exterior with zero further crossings through a <lipid bilayer>: enclosed spaces join when membranes fuse. Delivery therefore need not mean repeatedly pushing a folded <protein> through a membrane.

= Pancreatic secretory-protein pulse-chase experiment
{parent=Secretory protein routing}

In 1967, <James D. Jamieson> and <George E. Palade> incubated guinea pig <pancreas> slices, following a three-minute pulse of tritium-labeled <leucine> with excess unlabeled <leucine>. This <pulse-chase analysis> marked newly made <proteins> while reducing subsequent incorporation. Fixed, Epon-embedded thin sections were mounted on coated grids, covered with Ilford L-4 photographic emulsion, developed and stained for <electron microscopy>. <Autoradiography> located photographic grains relative to <pancreatic acinar cell> structures.<Intracellular Transport of Secretory Proteins in the Pancreatic Exocrine Cell. II. Transport to Condensing Vacuoles and Zymogen Granules by James D. Jamieson and George E. Palade>[Jamieson and Palade, Intracellular Transport of Secretory Proteins in the Pancreatic Exocrine Cell. II. Transport to Condensing Vacuoles and Zymogen Granules (1967), p. 598 Methods; Figure 10: three-minute pulse]{ref}

Pulse-end samples provided a temporal reference: label lay mainly over <rough endoplasmic reticulum>; later samples shifted toward peripheral <Golgi apparatus> elements, condensing vacuoles and <secretory granules>. As a contamination check, isolated granule pellets were also examined by <autoradiography>: early pellet labeling chiefly marked condensing vacuoles. Grids from the compared pellet samples were coated together to control photographic processing. This supported transport of a previously labeled cohort rather than separate synthesis at each location. Fixed samples did not track individual <proteins>; localization was finite, and labeling was not absolutely exclusive to secretory cargo. The result concerns this preparation, not every <protein> in every <biological cell>.<Intracellular Transport of Secretory Proteins in the Pancreatic Exocrine Cell. II. Transport to Condensing Vacuoles and Zymogen Granules by James D. Jamieson and George E. Palade>[pp. 603–606 and Figures 10–13: changing localization; pp. 610–611 and Figures 14–15, Table III: pellet check and matched emulsion processing; p. 611 General Premises: nonexportable-protein labeling]{ref}

= Microsome
{parent=Endoplasmic reticulum}

A <microsome> is a closed <lipid bilayer> compartment produced during disruption of a <biological tissue>; <rough endoplasmic reticulum>-derived <microsomes> provide a sealed compartment for <protein targeting> experiments.

= Plastid
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

A <plastid> is an <organelle> of the lineage that includes <chloroplasts>. A <chloroplast> is a photosynthetic <plastid>; the terms are not interchangeable because some <plastids> are nonphotosynthetic. <Endosymbiotic theory> connects <plastid> origins to <cyanobacteria>, while <endosymbiotic gene transfer> and <organelle protein import> explain genetic and functional integration with the host.

= Apicoplast
{parent=Plastid}

An <apicoplast> is a reduced nonphotosynthetic plastid in many apicomplexan parasites. Retained biosynthetic outputs can make it essential after photosystems and photosynthetic carbon fixation are lost.

= Mitochondria
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

= Mitochondrion
{synonym}

A <mitochondrion> is an <organelle> with an <outer mitochondrial membrane> and an <inner mitochondrial membrane>. The latter encloses the <mitochondrial matrix> and folds into <cristae>; the <intermembrane space> lies on its opposite side. This architecture separates a chemical workspace from a membrane that couples <electron> transport to <ATP> formation.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/4.3 Eukaryotic Cells>[Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others; §4.3, Mitochondria and Figure 4.14: two membranes, matrix and cristae]{ref}

\Image[mitochondrial-compartments-and-crista-sides.png]
{title=Compartments of a <mitochondrion> and the two sides of a <crista>}
{description=Original simplified section and enlargement, not a micrograph or scale drawing. The yellow interior of each fold connects to the peripheral <intermembrane space>; green denotes <mitochondrial matrix>. Arrows distinguish outward <proton> pumping from return through <ATP synthase>. Shapes and arrows encode no measured dimensions, rates or <stoichiometry>.}

For an original location exercise, place soluble label A in the <mitochondrial matrix> and soluble label B in the interior of a <crista>. In the depicted geometry, B can reach the peripheral <intermembrane space> through the fold's opening without crossing a <lipid bilayer>. A must cross the <inner mitochondrial membrane> to reach either location. A fold projecting into the <mitochondrial matrix> therefore does not make its enclosed fluid part of the <mitochondrial matrix>. Following a continuous path through the colored regions proves the distinction; merely asking whether a label is “inside the <mitochondrion>” loses it.

The functional connection is equally spatial. The <mitochondrial respiratory chain> pumps <protons> from the <mitochondrial matrix> side across the <inner mitochondrial membrane>. Their return through <ATP synthase> can drive <ATP> formation. <Oxidative phosphorylation> develops the mechanism and <chemiosmosis> gives its <free energy> bookkeeping. Compartmentalization is useful because it lets a shared <electrochemical gradient> couple different <proteins> without direct transfer of a chemical intermediate between each pair.

Under aerobic conditions, <mitochondria> supply much of the <ATP> in many <eukaryotic> <biological cells>; the contribution varies with <biological tissue>, demand and <oxygen> supply. <Glycolysis> also makes <ATP> in the <cytosol>. Mature mammalian <red blood cells> lack <mitochondria>, and <bacteria> have no <mitochondria> at all. Neither all <ATP> production nor all <biological cells> fit the familiar mitochondrial picture.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§4.3, Mitochondria; §7.2, glycolysis; §40.2, Red Blood Cells: mature mammalian cells lack mitochondria]{ref}

<Mitochondria> descend from a bacterial <endosymbiosis> and retain a <genome> and <ribosomes>, although many required <proteins> are encoded by nuclear <genes>. <Endosymbiotic theory> develops the ancestry argument; <organelle protein import> explains delivery of nuclear-encoded <proteins>. A functioning isolated <mitochondrion> demonstrates biochemical autonomy under supplied conditions, rather than independently establishing that ancestry.

= Mitochondrial fission-fusion cycle
{parent=Mitochondria}

Dynamic remodeling of a mitochondrial population through division and merger. Fusion permits content mixing, while fission supports distribution and can segregate portions for <mitophagy>.

= Inner mitochondrial membrane
{parent=Mitochondria}

The <inner mitochondrial membrane> separates <mitochondrial matrix> from <intermembrane space>. Its <selective permeability> permits distinct chemical conditions, while embedded <mitochondrial respiratory chain> <proteins> and <ATP synthase> support <oxidative phosphorylation>. <Cristae> increase its <surface area>.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others>[Chapter 14, The Mitochondrion Contains an Outer Membrane, an Inner Membrane, and Two Internal Compartments; Figure 14-8]{ref}

A selective boundary and a productive <enzyme> have different jobs. Even active respiratory <enzymes> cannot sustain a useful <electrochemical gradient> if <protons> return freely across the boundary. Conversely, an intact boundary without an <electron donor> does not supply the <energy> for continued pumping. The <isolated mitochondria respiration experiment> separates some of these dependencies by additions to the same preparation.

= Mitochondrial membrane potential
{parent=Inner mitochondrial membrane}
{title2=$\Delta\psi$}

The <electric potential> difference across the <inner mitochondrial membrane>, contributing to the <proton-motive force>. A change in potential can alter both useful phosphorylation and leak behavior.

= Mitochondrial phosphate carrier
{parent=Inner mitochondrial membrane}

A transport <protein> supplying <phosphate> across the <inner mitochondrial membrane> for processes including <ATP> synthesis.

= Adenine nucleotide translocator
{parent=Inner mitochondrial membrane}

A carrier exchanging adenine nucleotides across the <inner mitochondrial membrane>, supplying matrix <ADP> and exporting synthesized <ATP> during ordinary oxidative phosphorylation.

= Mitochondrial dicarboxylate carrier
{parent=Inner mitochondrial membrane}

A transport <protein> exchanging suitable dicarboxylates, including <succinate> and <malate>, with counter-substrates such as <phosphate>. Its relevance to respiration depends on the fuel and preparation.

= Crista
{parent=Inner mitochondrial membrane}
{wiki}

= Cristae
{synonym}

A <crista> is a fold of the <inner mitochondrial membrane> into the <mitochondrial matrix>. Its membrane remains continuous with the surrounding <inner mitochondrial membrane>; it is not a separately inserted sac like a <thylakoid>.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others>[Chapter 12, The Transport of Proteins into Mitochondria and Chloroplasts, Figure 12-22: continuity of cristae and contrast with thylakoids]{ref}

The diagram idealizes access through the fold's opening. Connectivity alone does not imply instant mixing or identical <concentrations> throughout the <intermembrane space>. Narrow geometry and molecular interactions can matter to transport. Increasing <surface area> also does not, by itself, prove a proportional increase in <ATP> production: the supply of <substrates>, available <oxygen> and the state of the coupling machinery must also support the flux.

= Mitochondrial matrix
{parent=Mitochondria}

The <mitochondrial matrix> is the compartment enclosed by the <inner mitochondrial membrane>, containing mitochondrial <DNA>, <ribosomes> and many <enzymes>. Most <citric acid cycle> steps occur here; <succinate dehydrogenase> is membrane-associated. The <citric acid cycle> supplies <NADH> for the <mitochondrial respiratory chain>.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others>[Chapter 14, The Mitochondrion Contains an Outer Membrane, an Inner Membrane, and Two Internal Compartments; Figure 14-8, matrix enzymes and inner-membrane succinate dehydrogenase]{ref}

Distinguish the location of a <chemical reaction> from the location of its detection. An external <oxygen> <electrode> can report consumption by an intact preparation without entering the <mitochondrial matrix>. That signal does not locate every intermediate, establish <protein targeting> or directly count newly formed <ATP>.

= Intermembrane space
{parent=Mitochondria}

In a <mitochondrion>, the <intermembrane space> lies between the <outer mitochondrial membrane> and <inner mitochondrial membrane>, extending into the interiors of <cristae>. It includes the side of the <inner mitochondrial membrane> from which <protons> return through <ATP synthase> to the <mitochondrial matrix>.

A useful sidedness check is to follow a single arrow: outward pumping goes from green to yellow in the diagram, while coupled return goes from yellow to green. These are opposing movements of <protons>, not a circuit in which <electrons> return through <ATP synthase>. The <mitochondrial respiratory chain> and <chemiosmosis> articles distinguish the two ledgers.

= Outer mitochondrial membrane
{parent=Mitochondria}

The <outer mitochondrial membrane> separates the surrounding <cytosol> from the <intermembrane space>. Channels permit passage of many small <molecules>, while the <inner mitochondrial membrane> supplies the main selective barrier between them and the <mitochondrial matrix>.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others>[Chapter 14, The Mitochondrion Contains an Outer Membrane, an Inner Membrane, and Two Internal Compartments: outer-membrane channels and inner-membrane selectivity]{ref}

Small-solute access does not imply unrestricted access for <proteins>. The <cytochrome c> addition in the <isolated mitochondria respiration experiment> tests a consequence of damage to this boundary. <Organelle protein import> describes purposeful delivery across mitochondrial boundaries.

= Isolated mitochondria respiration experiment
{parent=Mitochondria}

<Respiratory control> can be investigated outside an intact <biological cell> by supplying isolated <mitochondria> with <substrates> and <ADP>. This makes the boundary between <oxygen> consumption and <ATP> formation experimentally accessible. The historical <mitochondrial oxygen electrode assay> describes the 1955 experiment; the following authored protocol adds modern preparation and inhibitor controls.

In Functional Assessment of Isolated Mitochondria In Vitro (2009), <Ian R. Lanza> and <K. Sreekumaran Nair> describe human skeletal-muscle biopsy mincing, cold <protease> treatment, homogenization and <differential centrifugation>. A low-speed spin removes coarse material; subsequent spins collect and wash <mitochondria>. A stirred, closed $2\,\mathrm{mL}$ Oxygraph-2k chamber at $37\,{}^\circ\mathrm{C}$ uses a calibrated polarographic <oxygen> <electrode>. Its signal gives dissolved <oxygen> <concentration>; the negative time <derivative> gives consumption. Instrument corrections include electrode consumption and <oxygen> leakage.<Methods in Enzymology, volume 457: Mitochondrial Function, Part B/Functional Assessment of Isolated Mitochondria In Vitro>[Methods in Enzymology, volume 457: Mitochondrial Function, Part B; Tissue homogenization; Separation of mitochondria by differential centrifugation; Mitochondrial respiration, Overview; An oxygraph protocol for mitochondrial assessment, steps 1–2]{ref}

Their additions include <glutamate> plus <malate>, an <ADP> pulse, then saturating <ADP>. Added <cytochrome c> tests outer-membrane damage: stimulation suggests loss of endogenous <cytochrome c>. Later <oligomycin> inhibits <ATP synthase>; <FCCP> produces <respiratory uncoupling>; <antimycin A> inhibits <respiratory complex III> to estimate residual background. Figure 6 includes intervening <succinate dehydrogenase> and <respiratory complex I> interventions; the modeled traces below simplify that sequence. Isolation can damage or select <mitochondria>, and saturating assay conditions differ from those in living <biological tissue>.<Methods in Enzymology, volume 457: Mitochondrial Function, Part B/Functional Assessment of Isolated Mitochondria In Vitro>[Methods in Enzymology, volume 457: Mitochondrial Function, Part B; Figure 3; An oxygraph protocol for mitochondrial assessment, steps 3–11 and Figure 6; Summary, nonphysiological assay conditions]{ref}

The control logic matters more than a single steep slope. If <ADP> accelerates consumption and a finite pulse is followed by slowing, demand is affecting respiratory flux. If <oligomycin> slows consumption, but subsequent <FCCP> accelerates it, the <electron transport chain> can still turn over while <ATP synthase> remains inhibited. That contrast separates restricted coupled turnover from a completely inactive chain. Failure of <FCCP> to restore a rate is less specific: <substrate> limitation, damage, low <oxygen> or excessive uncoupler can also restrict respiration.

Neither the <cytochrome c> test alone nor a large <respiratory control ratio> establishes every aspect of mitochondrial integrity. Likewise, <oxygen> consumption is not a direct <ATP> assay: <respiratory uncoupling> provides a concrete counterexample. The preparation, additions and normalization denominator determine what comparison the experiment can support.

= Modeled mitochondrial oxygen traces
{parent=Isolated mitochondria respiration experiment}
{title2=$q=-V\,dc_{\mathrm{O}_2}/dt$}

The following original model chooses piecewise constant <oxygen> consumption rates in a closed, well-mixed $2\,\mathrm{mL}$ suspension. It assumes no gas exchange, negligible addition <volume (thermodynamics)>, a corrected sensor and sufficient <oxygen> throughout. The curves are invented teaching examples, not measured traces, fitted data or a reconstruction of Figure 6.

\Image[mitochondrial-modeled-oxygen-and-control-readouts.png]
{title=MODELED <oxygen> traces: a faster decline need not mean more <ATP>}
{description=Original piecewise linear <concentration> curve and corresponding consumption-rate plot. All rates and times are chosen. The finite first <ADP> pulse is stipulated to be used by minute 2; the second supplies excess <ADP>. <Oligomycin> remains present after <FCCP>. Substrate additions and intermediate chain interventions from the published protocol are omitted. The residual after <antimycin A> is shown as background, not <ATP> production.}

Start at $c(0)=220\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$. On successive one-minute intervals, choose positive slope magnitudes $5,25,5,25,6,32,1\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}\,min^{-1}}$. Integration gives the endpoint <concentrations> $220,215,190,185,160,154,122,121\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$. This is just the recurrence $c_{i+1}=c_i-r_i\Delta t$, with $\Delta t=1\,\mathrm{min}$; the step plot shows $r_i=-dc/dt$. The trace remains above zero by construction.

During the second <ADP> interval, the total consumption rate is
$$
q=-V\frac{dc_{\mathrm{O}_2}}{dt}
=(0.002\,\mathrm L)(25\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}\,min^{-1}})
=50\,\mathrm{nmol\,min^{-1}}.
$$
With a stipulated mitochondrial <protein> mass of $0.20\,\mathrm{mg}$, that is $250\,\mathrm{nmol\,min^{-1}\,mg^{-1}}$. Using a denominator twice as large would halve this reported rate without changing the raw <oxygen> trace. Comparisons therefore need both loading and the chosen denominator.

For a second bookkeeping check, the modeled post-<antimycin A> slope of $1\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}\,min^{-1}}$ corresponds to $2\,\mathrm{nmol\,min^{-1}}$ background. If that background is additive and unchanged in earlier windows, subtraction gives $48$, $10$ and $62\,\mathrm{nmol\,min^{-1}}$ for excess <ADP>, <oligomycin> and <FCCP>, respectively. The <oligomycin>-sensitive difference is $48-10=38\,\mathrm{nmol\,min^{-1}}$. It is a difference in <oxygen> consumption under the chosen interventions, not an <ATP> production rate. Converting it into one requires additional coupling assumptions or an independent <ATP> measurement; the <FCCP> interval shows why slope alone cannot supply that conversion.

Across the whole seven-minute example, consumption is $V(c(0)-c(7))=198\,\mathrm{nmol}$, also the sum of the seven interval consumptions. This equality checks the rate-to-amount accounting independently of how the intervals are named. Actual records require attention to calibration, mixing transients, background stability and decreasing <oxygen> availability; a line that steepens or flattens alone does not identify the cause.

= Golgi apparatus
{c}
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

The <Golgi apparatus> modifies and sorts cargo arriving from the <endoplasmic reticulum> for delivery to other cellular destinations. The eponym refers to <Camillo Golgi>.

Its usual stacked organization is polarized: cargo enters on the cis side, encounters medial processing regions, and leaves through the trans side and <trans-Golgi network>. <Golgi cisternae> enclose a <Golgi lumen> separated from the surrounding <cytosol>. Processing changes cargo; sorting selects its destination.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/The Golgi Apparatus>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Organization of the Golgi, Figures 9.22–9.23; Protein Sorting and Export from the Golgi Apparatus]{ref}

This division of labor explains why reaching the <Golgi apparatus> does not imply being secreted: cargo can take different onward routes, while resident <enzymes> continue working there. The conventional route discussed here is part of <secretory protein routing>; many <cytosol> <proteins> never enter it.

\Image[golgi-stack-cargo-processing-and-sidedness.png]
{title=<Golgi apparatus> polarity, processing and membrane sides}
{description=Original schematic of a stack and a local membrane enlargement. The spaces between <Golgi cisternae> contain <cytosol>, while each enclosed sac contains <Golgi lumen>. Purple cargo is soluble; the orange <membrane protein> has a lumen-facing <carbohydrate> chain and a cytosolic tail. Arrows show functional ordering, not a measured trajectory or a universal carrier mechanism. The chain-processing symbols are a constructed example.}

= Golgi cisterna
{c}
{parent=Golgi apparatus}

= Golgi cisternae
{c}
{synonym}

A <Golgi cisterna> is a flattened membrane-enclosed sac. Stacking several sacs does not make their interiors one open space: membrane separates <Golgi lumen> from intervening <cytosol>.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/The Golgi Apparatus>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Organization of the Golgi, Figure 9.22: membrane-enclosed cisternae]{ref}

= Golgi lumen
{c}
{parent=Golgi cisterna}

The <Golgi lumen> is the enclosed interior of a <Golgi cisterna>, distinct from the surrounding <cytosol>.

The membrane-sidedness rule in <secretory protein routing> still applies within the <Golgi apparatus>. For a local worked example, stipulate a <membrane protein> with a <carbohydrate> chain exposed to <Golgi lumen> and a tail exposed to <cytosol>. An <enzyme> confined to <Golgi lumen> can contact that chain, but cannot directly contact the cytosolic tail without a boundary-crossing step. A change in which <Golgi cisterna> contains the <protein> need not turn the <protein> over. Thus chemical accessibility and cargo location are different questions: moving cargo onward preserves its membrane sides under the budding and fusion operations described in <secretory protein routing>.

= Golgi cargo processing
{c}
{parent=Golgi apparatus}

<Golgi cargo processing> includes <enzyme>-mediated remodeling of attached <oligosaccharides>. <Glycosylation> can involve addition of <sugars>, while processing can also remove them. Different <Golgi apparatus> regions have different processing <enzymes>; their <substrate> requirements constrain the order of possible <chemical reactions>.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Transport from the ER through the Golgi Apparatus>[Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others; Oligosaccharide Chains Are Processed in the Golgi Apparatus; The Golgi Cisternae Are Organized as a Series of Processing Compartments, Figure 13-28]{ref}

An original model makes the importance of ordering explicit. Write a toy <carbohydrate> chain as $XA$, read with its terminal symbol on the right. Define a trimming <function> $T$ that removes a terminal $A$, and an extension <function> $E$ that adds $B$ only when the terminal symbol is $X$. In every other case the corresponding <function> leaves the chain unchanged. Then
$$
XA\xrightarrow{T}X\xrightarrow{E}XB,\qquad XA\xrightarrow{E}XA\xrightarrow{T}X.
$$
In particular, $E(T(XA))=XB$ but $T(E(XA))=X$: the two <functions> do not commute. Each result follows by inspecting the terminal symbol before applying the stipulated rule. These symbols are artificial labels, rather than a natural <oligosaccharide> sequence or measured <enzyme> pathway. The example explains why “two processing steps occurred” does not determine the product unless their order and <substrate> conditions are also specified. In the <Golgi apparatus>, spatial organization can help arrange successive opportunities for processing.

= trans-Golgi network
{parent=Golgi apparatus}

= TGN
{c}
{synonym}

The <trans-Golgi network> is the sorting and export region at the trans side of the <Golgi apparatus>. Onward traffic can supply the <cell membrane>, <secretory granules>, or the <lysosome> route.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/The Golgi Apparatus>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Protein Sorting and Export from the Golgi Apparatus, Figure 9.27 and lysosomal targeting discussion]{ref}

Processing and sorting answer different questions. In a constructed example, two cargo <proteins> could receive the same <carbohydrate> modification yet carry different targeting information and leave in different <transport vesicles>. Conversely, two chemically different cargo <proteins> could share a destination. A processing readout alone therefore cannot establish the final address; an experiment must also identify cargo location or delivery. Return traffic and retention likewise matter: the <Golgi apparatus> is a working compartment, not merely a one-way queue that empties all of its <enzymes> into outgoing carriers.

= Cisternal maturation
{parent=Golgi apparatus}

In <cisternal maturation>, a cargo-containing <Golgi cisterna> changes from early to later identity as its resident <proteins> change; retrieval of resident <enzymes> toward earlier compartments helps maintain <Golgi apparatus> polarity. In the contrasting stable-compartment model, cargo moves between persistent <Golgi cisternae> in <transport vesicles>. These mechanisms need not be mutually exclusive.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Transport from the ER through the Golgi Apparatus>[Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others; Transport Through the Golgi Apparatus May Occur by Vesicular Transport or Cisternal Maturation, Figure 13-30 and concluding comparison]{ref}

The distinction is between following cargo and following its container. A cargo label encountered first in an early processing environment and later in a late one is compatible with either changing containers or a container changing identity. Finding <transport vesicles> nearby does not by itself establish which cargo they carry or their direction of travel. Testing <cisternal maturation> therefore requires evidence about compartment identity and continuity as well as cargo position; a single mechanism should not be assumed for every cargo and <biological cell>.

= Golgi tracer interpretation
{c}
{parent=Golgi apparatus}

The <pancreatic secretory-protein pulse-chase experiment> of <James D. Jamieson> and <George E. Palade> used guinea pig <pancreas> slices and a short pulse of tritium-labeled <leucine> followed by a chase. Thin sections on coated grids received Ilford L-4 emulsion; developed grains were read against structures by <electron microscopy>. Their Table II localized label to peripheral <Golgi apparatus> vesicles and condensing vacuoles, without significant labeling of the piled flattened <Golgi cisternae>.<Intracellular Transport of Secretory Proteins in the Pancreatic Exocrine Cell. II. Transport to Condensing Vacuoles and Zymogen Granules by James D. Jamieson and George E. Palade>[p. 598 Methods; p. 609 Table II and its footnote]{ref}

An important comparison checked the isolated granule fraction by <autoradiography> and <electron microscopy>: early labeling came substantially from contaminating condensing vacuoles, rather than mature <secretory granules>. Thus a fraction's name did not uniquely identify the labeled structure. This supported transport into the <Golgi apparatus>'s condensing region and onward storage, while fixed samples and finite grain localization limited interpretation. It did not establish a resolved cis-to-trans itinerary across every <Golgi cisterna>. The preparation's labeling also did not mark secretory cargo exclusively.<Intracellular Transport of Secretory Proteins in the Pancreatic Exocrine Cell. II. Transport to Condensing Vacuoles and Zymogen Granules by James D. Jamieson and George E. Palade>[p. 609 Figure 14; p. 613, Transport of Labeled Proteins to Condensing Vacuoles, Zymogen Granules, and the Acinar Lumen: comparison of fraction labeling with microscopy; p. 611, General Premises]{ref}

\Image[golgi-tracing-cargo-versus-cisterna-identity.png]
{title=<Golgi tracer interpretation>: cargo position versus compartment identity}
{description=Original interpretation diagram motivated by <pulse-chase analysis>. The upper panel explains the granule-fraction comparison in the inspected experiment; it gives no counts or time course. The lower panels are constructed alternatives: the same cargo can encounter early and late identities by transfer between stable <Golgi cisternae> or by <cisternal maturation>. The colored identities and cargo symbols are illustrative, not observed fluorescence or reconstructed micrographs.}

For a minimal worked inference, record only two outcomes for one labeled cargo cohort: early identity first, late identity later. Model V transfers cargo from container $C_1$ to container $C_2$; model M keeps it in $C_1$ while $C_1$ changes identity. Both produce the same recorded outcomes, so those outcomes alone cannot distinguish V from M. The proof is constructive: exhibit both histories and discard container identity from each record. More informative tracking would retain both cargo and resident-compartment markers, while resolving whether the same compartment persists. This is why <pulse-chase analysis> and compartment tracking answer complementary questions about the <Golgi apparatus>.

= Transport vesicle
{parent=Organelle}

A <transport vesicle> is a membrane-bounded carrier that moves cargo between cellular compartments. Budding and fusion preserve the distinction between lumen-facing and <cytosol>-facing membrane surfaces.

= Extracellular vesicle
{parent=Transport vesicle}

A membrane-enclosed particle released by a cell into the extracellular environment. Isolation methods and cargo characterisation determine what signalling or transfer claims can be made.

= Lysosome
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

A <lysosome> is an acidic digestive and recycling <organelle>: one surrounding <lipid bilayer> encloses a <lysosomal lumen> containing <acid hydrolases>. These <enzymes> break down <proteins>, <nucleic acids>, <carbohydrates> and <lipids> by <hydrolysis>. Cargo can originate outside the <biological cell>, entering through <endocytosis>, or within its own <cytoplasm>, delivered through <autophagy>. Digestion therefore serves both disposal and reuse.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/Lysosomes>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Cooper, The Cell, 2nd edition (2000), Lysosomes: opening paragraph; Lysosomal Acid Hydrolases; Phagocytosis and Autophagy, Figure 9.37]{ref}

\Image[lysosome-compartment-and-cargo-routes.png]
{title=<Lysosome> compartment, cargo delivery and recycling}
{description=Original schematic: <endocytosis> and <macroautophagy> supply material for digestion, while <secretory protein routing> supplies digestive <enzymes>. The enclosure at the right distinguishes <lysosomal lumen> from <cytosol>; arrows indicate routes, not measured rates. Shapes, sizes and cargo counts are illustrative.}

An <endosome> sorts endocytosed cargo: some returns to the <cell membrane>, while some proceeds toward <lysosomes>. An <autophagosome> instead encloses material already in the <cytoplasm>. Both routes bring cargo into contact with digestive <enzymes>; neither implies that every internalized <molecule> is destroyed.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Transport from the Trans Golgi Network to Lysosomes>[Alberts and others, Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002), Multiple Pathways Deliver Materials to Lysosomes, Figure 13-35]{ref}

Enzyme supply is a different itinerary from cargo disposal. <Secretory protein routing> and <protein targeting> deliver newly made digestive <enzymes> through the <endoplasmic reticulum>, <Golgi apparatus> and <trans-Golgi network> toward <lysosomes>.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/The Golgi Apparatus>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Protein Sorting and Export from the Golgi Apparatus, lysosomal enzyme targeting]{ref} The existing <membrane transport> and <differential centrifugation> articles explain the boundary-crossing and separation principles used below.

A <proteasome> is a different digestive machine: a <protein> complex without a surrounding <lipid bilayer>, acting on individual <proteins> in the <cytosol> and <cell nucleus>. Many targets carry <ubiquitin> chains. A <lysosome> accepts a much broader range of cargo, including material delivered inside an <autophagosome>. Thus an unwanted soluble <protein> and an unwanted <mitochondrion> pose different disposal problems; a <proteasome> does not swallow an intact <mitochondrion>.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/Protein Degradation>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Cooper, The Cell, 2nd edition (2000), The Ubiquitin-Proteasome Pathway, Figure 7.39; Lysosomal Proteolysis, Figure 7.41]{ref}

= Lysosomal lumen
{parent=Lysosome}

The <lysosomal lumen> is the aqueous interior of a <lysosome>, separated from <cytosol> by its surrounding <lipid bilayer>. A membrane pump couples <ATP> <hydrolysis> to inward transport of <hydrogen ions>, maintaining an acidic environment for <acid hydrolases>. <Membrane transport proteins> export digestion products, including <amino acids> and <sugars>, for reuse in <metabolism>. Compartmentalization combines restricted access with controlled exchange.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Transport from the Trans Golgi Network to Lysosomes>[Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others; Lysosomes Are the Principal Sites of Intracellular Digestion, Figure 13-31: ATP-driven acidification and export of digestion products]{ref}

= Autophagy
{parent=Lysosome}
{wiki}

<Autophagy> delivers a <biological cell>'s own material for digestion and recycling in <lysosomes>. <Macroautophagy> is the enclosing-carrier route illustrated here; other forms deliver cargo differently. The cargo's internal origin distinguishes this route from uptake by <endocytosis>.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/Protein Degradation>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Lysosomal Proteolysis, Figure 7.41 and subsequent selective-uptake paragraph: enclosed cargo and alternative uptake]{ref}

= Mitophagy
{parent=Autophagy}

Selective turnover of <mitochondria> or mitochondrial portions through <autophagy>. Mitochondrial fragmentation alone is not proof of successful turnover.

= Macroautophagy
{parent=Autophagy}

In <macroautophagy>, a portion of <cytoplasm>, potentially including an <organelle>, is enclosed in an <autophagosome> before delivery to a <lysosome>. Sequestration precedes digestion.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Transport from the Trans Golgi Network to Lysosomes>[Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others; Multiple Pathways Deliver Materials to Lysosomes, autophagy paragraph and Figure 13-35]{ref}

= Autophagosome
{parent=Macroautophagy}
{wiki}

An <autophagosome> is the membrane-enclosed carrier formed around <macroautophagy> cargo. Enclosing a <mitochondrion> does not immediately digest it: subsequent delivery must expose the cargo to digestive <enzymes> in <lysosomes>.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/Lysosomes>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Phagocytosis and Autophagy, autophagosome fusion and digestion, Figure 9.37]{ref}

= Lysosomal enzyme latency
{parent=Lysosome}

<Lysosomal enzyme latency> is the difference between the <enzyme> activity accessible to an added <substrate> before disruption of the surrounding membrane and the activity revealed after disruption. Low accessible activity can reflect separation of <enzyme> from <substrate>, rather than absence of <enzyme>. This is an assay-accessibility statement, not a claim that the intact <lysosome> is inactive in the living <biological cell>.<Exploring Cells with a Centrifuge by Christian de Duve>[Christian de Duve, Exploring Cells with a Centrifuge (Nobel Lecture, 12 December 1974), printed pp. 157–159, enzyme-latency discussion and Figure 6]{ref}

\Image[lysosome-latency-disruption-assay.png]
{title=<Lysosomal enzyme latency>: matched assay conditions and disruption controls}
{description=Original assay-logic diagram and explicitly chosen activity example. The values $10$, $100$ and $25$ are arbitrary blank-corrected rate units, not historical observations. The soluble-<enzyme> comparison is an additional control proposed for the schematic, not an asserted measurement from the <rat-liver acid-phosphatase latency experiment>.}

Let $F$ be the blank-corrected initial rate measured with a membrane-preserving preparation and $T>0$ the rate after a treatment that exposes all assayable <enzyme>. Use matched amounts of starting material, <pH>, <substrate> and measurement time. Define the apparent latent fraction by
$$
\ell=1-\frac{F}{T}.
$$
This definition requires more than a large treated signal. Disruption must preserve <enzyme> activity, and the treatment must not independently alter the readout. A soluble-<enzyme> comparison with and without treatment helps test direct activation or inhibition; an appropriate blank tests signal unrelated to <enzyme>-catalysed <hydrolysis>. These are controls for interpreting the constructed assay, rather than additional historical observations.

For a deliberately chosen example, take $F=10$ and $T=100$ arbitrary rate units. Then $\ell=0.90$: ninety percent of the assayable activity was initially inaccessible. After hypothetical partial damage, $F=25$ with unchanged $T=100$ gives $\ell=0.75$. The preparation has lost latency without gaining total assayable activity. These percentages describe activity accessibility; they do not count intact <lysosomes>. Unequal <enzyme> contents, partial leakage and mixed particle populations prevent that identification. If $F>T$, the formula is negative, flagging failure of this simple accessibility interpretation rather than a negative population of hidden <enzymes>.

To check the logic, divide the same uniformly mixed preparation into two equal portions. If dilution halves both measured rates, $(F/2)/(T/2)=F/T$, so $\ell$ is unchanged. In contrast, comparing an undiluted untreated portion with a diluted treated portion changes the ratio artificially. Normalization to the same amount of original material is therefore part of the definition, not an optional numerical correction.

= Rat-liver acid-phosphatase latency experiment
{parent=Lysosomal enzyme latency}

In the 1951 study by <Jacques Berthet> and <Christian de Duve>, <liver> from fasted rats was immediately chilled in an ice-cold isotonic <chemical solution>. The tissue was generally pressed through wire mesh; the weighed pulp was homogenized in cold $0.25\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <sucrose> using a glass <Potter-Elvehjem homogenizer>. Their <acid phosphatase> test concerned activity toward added <glycerophosphate> at <pH> $5$: the readout was <substrate> <hydrolysis>, rather than an image of a particle.<Tissue fractionation studies. 1. The existence of a mitochondria-linked, enzymically inactive form of acid phosphatase in rat-liver tissue by Jacques Berthet and Christian de Duve>[Berthet and de Duve (1951), Tissue fractionation studies. 1, printed p. 174, introduction and Methods: Fractionation of tissue constituents; inspected first-page text]{ref}

<Christian de Duve>'s retrospective describes successive <centrifugation> steps, separating <centrifugation pellets> and <supernatants>, and comparing <enzyme> distributions across the recovered fractions. Membrane disruption exposed previously inaccessible <acid phosphatase> activity, providing the crucial comparison with the membrane-preserving preparation. Joint fractionation patterns and release behavior of several <acid hydrolases> supported a shared population of digestive particles. By 1955, this evidence supported the <lysosome> concept.<Exploring Cells with a Centrifuge by Christian de Duve>[Exploring Cells with a Centrifuge, printed pp. 152–153: preparation and differential fractionation; pp. 156–159, Figures 4–7: enzyme distributions, disruption and latency]{ref}

The inference was biochemical: a barrier restricted access to active <enzymes>. Co-sedimentation could confuse distinct particles; a crude mitochondrial fraction did not establish residence inside <mitochondria>. <Lysosomal enzyme latency> neither images an <organelle> nor determines its shape. <Electron microscopy> of enriched fractions supplied separate morphological evidence.<Exploring Cells with a Centrifuge by Christian de Duve>[Printed pp. 153–157: mixed populations and distribution patterns; pp. 161–164: incomplete separation and subsequent electron microscopy of lysosome-rich fractions]{ref}

= Endosome
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

An <endosome> is a membrane-enclosed sorting compartment in the <endocytosis> pathway. Cargo may return to the <cell membrane> or proceed toward <lysosomes>; entry therefore does not by itself establish a digestive fate.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/Lysosomes>[The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper; Figure 9.36: early endosomes, recycling and onward maturation]{ref}

= Endosome maturation
{parent=Endosome}

Progressive changes in endosomal membrane identity and lumenal processing, accompanied by sorting and retrieval rather than uniform conversion of all endocytosed cargo into a degradative route.

= Late endosome
{parent=Endosome}

A maturing degradative compartment distinguished from early sorting endosomes by changes in proteins, lipids, lumenal conditions and fusion competence.

= Early endosome
{parent=Endosome}

An <early endosome> is an early sorting compartment for internalized material. Delivery to it, recycling from it and onward trafficking are experimentally distinct from uptake across the <plasma membrane>.

= Chloroplast
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <chloroplast> is a photosynthetic <plastid> in <plant> and <algae> <biological cells>. The green-plant <chloroplast> modeled here has a <chloroplast envelope> surrounding <chloroplast stroma> and internal <thylakoids>. <Light-dependent reactions> occur at the <thylakoid membrane>; the <Calvin cycle> operates in the <chloroplast stroma>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis>[Biology 2e, 8.1, Basic Photosynthetic Structures and The Two Parts of Photosynthesis]{ref}

<Cyanobacteria> perform <oxygenic photosynthesis> without <chloroplasts>: the <prokaryotic> <biological cell> itself contains the photosynthetic machinery. Thus <chloroplast> identifies an <organelle>, while <oxygenic photosynthesis> identifies a process. <Endosymbiotic theory> explains their historical connection; <organelle protein import> explains how many nuclear-encoded <proteins> reach the modern <organelle>.

\Image[chloroplast-envelope-thylakoid-boundary-map.png]
{title=<Chloroplast> compartments and boundary counting}
{description=Original simplified section and compartment-access model, not a micrograph or scale drawing. The enlargement separates <thylakoid lumen> from the <chloroplast stroma> between stacked sacs. The route counts crossings of membrane barriers, not molecular leaflets; actual permeability and transport mechanisms are omitted.}

The useful feature is selective separation: <enzymes> and <substrates> can share an <organelle> without sharing a compartment. For a constructed accessibility example, put a soluble <enzyme> in <chloroplast stroma> and its <substrate> in <thylakoid lumen>. Sharing the same <chloroplast> does not permit direct contact; contact requires a transport or boundary-disruption step. Placing the <substrate> between adjacent <thylakoids> instead puts it on the stromal side. The visual stack alone cannot establish chemical accessibility.

For the simplest boundary-counting model, represent surrounding <cytosol>, <chloroplast intermembrane space>, <chloroplast stroma> and <thylakoid lumen> by four <vertices> in a chain. Its three <edges> represent the outer envelope, inner envelope and <thylakoid membrane>, respectively. A route from <cytosol> to <chloroplast stroma> crosses two barriers; extending it into <thylakoid lumen> adds a third. This follows by listing the <edges> along the chain. Counting the two leaflets of each <lipid bilayer> separately would answer a different question. These counts describe this idealized geometry, not the number of <protein targeting> signals or the transport itinerary of every <protein>.

The <light-dependent reactions> article develops <ATP>, <NADPH> and <electrochemical gradient> coupling, and the <Calvin cycle> article develops <carbon fixation> bookkeeping. Their different locations make the <chloroplast> interesting as an organized chemical workspace. Light-independent <chemical reactions> still depend on supplied carriers and regulation; the name does not prescribe nighttime operation.

= Chloroplast gene expression
{parent=Chloroplast}

Production and regulation of functional <RNAs> and <proteins> from the <chloroplast genome>. The location of the regulated process and the genomic origin of its regulator are different properties.

= GUN1
{parent=Chloroplast gene expression}
{c}

A nuclear-encoded plastid factor implicated in coordination of plastid state with nuclear <gene expression>. It should not be identified with every exported plastid stress signal.<Sigma factor-mediated plastid retrograde signals control nuclear gene expression by Jesse D. Woodson and others>[Abstract and Results]{ref}

= Retrograde signaling
{parent=Chloroplast gene expression}

Regulation of nuclear <gene expression> in response to the state of an <organelle>. The identity and route of a signal require experimental evidence beyond correlation of nuclear and organellar changes.

= Anterograde signaling
{parent=Chloroplast gene expression}

Regulation directed from the nuclear system toward an <organelle>, including control of imported regulators. It contrasts with <retrograde signaling> in direction, not necessarily in timescale.

= Nuclear-encoded plastid RNA polymerase
{parent=Chloroplast gene expression}

= NEP
{synonym}
{c}

A nuclear-specified plastid <RNA polymerase> of the phage-type family, with promoter preferences distinct from <plastid-encoded RNA polymerase>.

= Plastid-encoded RNA polymerase
{parent=Chloroplast gene expression}

= PEP
{synonym}
{c}

A bacterial-type plastid <RNA polymerase> with core subunits encoded by the plastid <genome>. Nuclear-encoded <sigma factors> and associated components mean that its name does not imply autonomous regulation.

= Chloroplast envelope
{parent=Chloroplast}

The double boundary formed by the <outer chloroplast envelope membrane> and <inner chloroplast envelope membrane>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis>[Biology 2e, 8.1, Figure 8.6: double membrane envelope]{ref}

= Outer chloroplast envelope membrane
{parent=Chloroplast envelope}

Separates surrounding <cytosol> from <chloroplast intermembrane space>.

= Inner chloroplast envelope membrane
{parent=Chloroplast envelope}

Separates <chloroplast intermembrane space> from <chloroplast stroma>.

= Chloroplast intermembrane space
{parent=Chloroplast envelope}

The space between the two <chloroplast envelope> membranes.

= Chloroplast stroma
{parent=Chloroplast}

The <chloroplast stroma> is the fluid compartment inside the <inner chloroplast envelope membrane> and outside <thylakoids>, containing <Calvin cycle> <enzymes>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis>[Biology 2e, 8.1, Figures 8.6–8.7: stroma and Calvin cycle location]{ref}

A <chloroplast stroma> location is therefore more informative than an unspecified “inside the <chloroplast>” location. In the boundary model above, entering <chloroplast stroma> has not yet entered <thylakoid lumen>. <Protein targeting> must distinguish these destinations; a fraction containing <chloroplasts> does not establish which internal compartment contains a detected <protein>.

= Thylakoid
{parent=Chloroplast}
{wiki}

A <thylakoid> is a membrane-enclosed sac; its <thylakoid membrane> contains <chlorophyll> and <light-dependent reactions> machinery. A stack is a <granum>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis>[Biology 2e, 8.1, Basic Photosynthetic Structures, Figure 8.6]{ref}

The membrane and its enclosed fluid answer different location questions. A soluble <protein> in <thylakoid lumen> is inside the sac; a <membrane protein> is embedded in its boundary and can expose different parts on the two sides. <Membrane topology> and <organelle protein import> provide the reusable language for those distinctions.

= Thylakoid membrane
{parent=Thylakoid}

Separates <thylakoid lumen> from <chloroplast stroma> in a <chloroplast>.

= Thylakoid lumen
{parent=Thylakoid}

The fluid interior enclosed by the <thylakoid membrane>.

= Granum
{parent=Thylakoid}

= Grana
{synonym}

A stack of <thylakoids>.

= Hill reaction
{c}
{parent=Chloroplast}
{wiki}

The <Hill reaction> couples <light>-driven <electron> transfer from <water> to an added <electron acceptor> with <molecular oxygen> release in an isolated photosynthetic preparation. The <chloroplast dye-reduction experiment> uses <DCPIP>.<Investigating the light dependent reaction in photosynthesis at Practical Biology>[Investigating the light dependent reaction in photosynthesis, introductory and Teaching notes: artificial acceptor assay]{ref}

In 1937, <Robin Hill>'s Oxygen Evolved by Isolated Chloroplasts described a spectroscopic <molecular oxygen> measurement in illuminated isolated <chloroplasts>: oxygenation of muscle <hemoglobin> provided the indicator. This was an oxygen-binding readout. The publicly accessible first paragraph establishes that method; it does not specify enough preparation or control details to reconstruct the entire historical experiment here.<Oxygen Evolved by Isolated Chloroplasts by Robin Hill>[Hill, Nature 139, 881–882 (22 May 1937), public first-paragraph preview: hemoglobin oxygenation and spectroscopic saturation measurement]{ref}

The experimental idea is powerful because isolation asks how much of <photosynthesis> remains functional outside an intact <biological cell>. Breaking a <chloroplast envelope> does not logically imply destroying every internal <thylakoid membrane>. Conversely, finding an active preparation cannot establish that its <chloroplast envelope> remains intact. Activity and structural integrity require different evidence.

= Chloroplast dye-reduction experiment
{parent=Hill reaction}

The Practical Biology protocol uses cold <sucrose> in <buffer solution>, ground green leaves, muslin filtration, <centrifugation> and cold pellet resuspension. A $100\,\mathrm{W}$ lamp illuminates extract plus <DCPIP> from $12$–$15\,\mathrm{cm}$; a red-filter colorimeter monitors blue-color loss.<Investigating the light dependent reaction in photosynthesis at Practical Biology>[Apparatus and Chemicals; Technical note 2; Procedure a–k; Sample results, Using a bench centrifuge: EEL colorimeter with red filter]{ref}

Compare extract plus dye in darkness, illuminated dye without extract, extract without dye, and <supernatant> plus dye: controls for <light>, extract dependence, background color and incomplete separation. Green <supernatant> can remain active; a <centrifugation pellet> establishes neither purity nor intact <chloroplasts>.<Investigating the light dependent reaction in photosynthesis at Practical Biology>[Procedure i–k, five-tube table; Teaching notes; Questions 1–3 and answers: control interpretations and green-supernatant limitation]{ref}

\Image[chloroplast-hill-dye-controls-and-readouts.png]
{title=<Hill reaction>: preparation, comparisons and distinct readouts}
{description=Original experimental-logic schematic. Control outcomes are conditional interpretations, not invented observations. The modern dye assay follows the inspected Practical Biology protocol; the separate historical branch denotes <hemoglobin> oxygenation. No measured time course, apparatus photograph or published figure is reproduced.}

Here is an original logical check of the controls. Suppose only the illuminated extract-plus-dye condition loses blue color, while the dark mixture and illuminated dye-only mixture retain it. Removing <light> or removing extract each removes the effect in those comparisons. That supports a joint requirement under the tested conditions. If the dye-only mixture also loses blue color, the proposed attribution to extract has failed its control; a faster change in the extract mixture would require a quantitative comparison before attribution. These are conditional cases, not experimental results.

Even the favorable case leaves three distinct questions. Dye <reduction> demonstrates delivery of <electrons> to an artificial <electron acceptor>. An independently calibrated <molecular oxygen> readout is needed to measure <molecular oxygen> evolution. Demonstrating <carbon fixation> additionally requires evidence that <carbon dioxide> becomes incorporated into organic material. None of the latter quantities is supplied by a blue-color endpoint alone. An <absorbance> change also needs background and scattering controls before it can be treated as dye <concentration>; <Beer-Lambert law> states the relevant assumptions.

In particular, two hypothetical preparations can give the same dye endpoint while differing in whether their stromal <Calvin cycle> is functional. Observing only the dye discards that distinction. The counterexample establishes why dye decolorization cannot alone prove complete <photosynthesis>, <carbon fixation>, or an intact <chloroplast>. <Temperature>, extract loading and illumination must also be matched when comparing rates: a lamp can alter both <light> exposure and heating.

= Cell nucleus
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cell nucleus> is a membrane-bounded compartment characteristic of <eukaryotic> <biological cells>, containing most of the cellular <DNA> in a typical nucleated <biological cell>. Its double <nuclear envelope> separates this <DNA> from the surrounding <cytoplasm>. Most does not mean all: <mitochondria> and <chloroplasts> retain their own <DNA>. Nor does every specialized <eukaryotic> <biological cell> retain a <cell nucleus>: mature mammalian <red blood cells> have lost theirs. <Prokaryotes> organize their <DNA> in a <nucleoid> without a <nuclear envelope>, and still have <ribosomes>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleus[NHGRI: nucleus], https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 23.1: organelle DNA]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 40.2: mature mammalian red blood cells].

Follow one nuclear <protein>-coding <gene>: its <DNA> is used in <transcription> to make <RNA>; after the appropriate processing, <messenger RNA> is exported through a <nuclear pore complex>; a <ribosome> in the <cytoplasm> then uses it in <biological translation> to build an <amino acid> chain. The <DNA> remains in the <cell nucleus>. The <RNA> is a template, not the material converted into a <protein>. The <ribosome> diagram below follows this route; <gene expression> gives a complementary <nucleotide sequence> example. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al.: selective RNA export and protein import].

This architecture makes <gene expression> interesting in a spatial way: nuclear <transcription> and cytoplasmic <biological translation> are separated by a selective boundary. A <messenger RNA> can be processed before export and use, while <proteins> needed for nuclear functions travel inward. Inside the <cell nucleus>, the <cell nucleolus> helps assemble <ribosome> subunits for export. Thus the compartment housing most <DNA> also helps build the machinery that interprets its messages elsewhere.

Robert Brown described and named the <cell nucleus> in his account of <plant> <biological cells>, read to the Linnean Society in 1831 and published in its Transactions in 1833. This historical observation identified a recurring structure, without establishing its later-understood role in <DNA> storage or <gene expression>. See https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1095-8339.1829.tb00158.x[Brown, On the Organs and Mode of Fecundation in Orchideæ and Asclepiadeæ, Transactions 16, 685–738 (1833)]. This is a documented contribution rather than a claim that no one had observed the structure earlier.

= Nuclear retention
{parent=Cell nucleus}

= Nuclear lamina
{parent=Cell nucleus}

= Lamina-associated domain
{parent=Nuclear lamina}

= LAD
{synonym}
{c}

A genomic region with elevated association with the nuclear lamina under a specified measurement. <DamID> reports accumulated proximity rather than continuous attachment of every copy.

= Nuclear envelope
{parent=Cell nucleus}
{wiki}

The <nuclear envelope> is the double-membrane boundary around a <cell nucleus>. Regulated passage connects nuclear processes to the surrounding <cytoplasm>: <transcription> produces <RNA> in the <cell nucleus>, while exported <messenger RNA> can undergo <biological translation> on <ribosomes> outside it. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nuclear-Membrane[NHGRI: nuclear membrane].

= Nuclear pore complex
{parent=Nuclear envelope}
{wiki}

= Nuclear pore
{synonym}

A <nuclear pore complex> is an assembly of <proteins> spanning the <nuclear envelope> and regulating passage between the <cell nucleus> and <cytosol>. It is a selective gateway, not simply an unregulated hole: <messenger RNA> export and import of nuclear <proteins> require appropriate transport machinery. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al.: nuclear pore complexes].

The <nuclear pore complex> occupies a junction of both <nuclear envelope> membranes. Its <protein> scaffold surrounds an aqueous passage connecting <cytosol> to the <cell nucleus>, rather than the intermembrane space. <Diffusion> permits small soluble cargo to pass; selective receptors transport other cargo. <Molecular mass> alone is not a universal cutoff.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others>[Nuclear Pore Complexes Perforate the Nuclear Envelope, Figures 12-10–12-11; Nuclear Localization Signals Direct Nuclear Proteins to the Nucleus]{ref}

\Image[nuclear-pore-envelope-topology.png]
{title=The <nuclear pore complex> bridges both membranes of the <nuclear envelope>}
{description=Original topology schematic, not a measured structure. The central passage connects <cytosol> and <cell nucleus>; cargo does not enter the intermembrane space. Shapes, distances and arrows are illustrative.}

= Nuclear localization sequence
{parent=Nuclear pore complex}
{wiki}

= Nuclear localization signal
{synonym}

= NLS
{c}
{synonym}

A <nuclear localization sequence> is an ordered stretch of <amino acid residues> that can direct <protein> import into a <cell nucleus>. In the classical pathway, <importin> α recognizes the <nuclear localization sequence>, and <importin> β mediates passage through the <nuclear pore complex>. Other recognition pathways exist. An exposed <nuclear localization sequence> is a targeting feature, not a guarantee of import under every condition.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper>[Selective Transport of Proteins to and from the Nucleus, Figure 8.9; Regulation of Nuclear Protein Import]{ref}

<RNA> export is a different cargo pathway: <RNA> travels associated with <proteins>, as a <ribonucleoprotein>, rather than being imported by its own <nuclear localization sequence>. Export of <messenger RNA> connects nuclear <transcription> to cytoplasmic <biological translation>.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper>[Transport of RNAs, opening paragraphs]{ref}

= Nuclear pore localization experiment
{parent=Nuclear pore complex}

In a 1982 experiment, purified <nucleoplasmin> was labeled with radioactive <iodine> and cleaved by <proteolysis>. <Microinjection> delivered cargo into <Xenopus laevis> <oocyte> <cytoplasm>. Manually separated <cell nuclei> and <cytoplasm> were compared by <gel electrophoresis> and <autoradiography>; section <autoradiography> checked internal localization. Tail-bearing cargo entered the <cell nucleus>; tailless cores did not. Direct nuclear <microinjection> retained the cores: the tails mattered for entry, but the assay did not identify a receptor.<A polypeptide domain that specifies migration of nucleoplasmin into the nucleus by Colin Dingwall and others>[Cell 30, 449–458: Figures 2–3, 5 and 7; The Trypsin-resistant Core Remains in the Nucleus after Nuclear Injection]{ref}

\Image[nuclear-pore-localization-assay.png]
{title=Changing cargo and injection site separates nuclear entry from retention}
{description=Original qualitative summary of the <nucleoplasmin> comparisons above. Symbols represent localization, not measured counts or copied source images.}

Direct nuclear <microinjection> bypassed entry, distinguishing failure to enter from failure to remain inside a <cell nucleus>.

= Nuclear pore transport balance
{parent=Nuclear pore complex}
{title2=$R=C_n/C_c$}

Nuclear accumulation should compare <concentrations>, not just total cargo. In an original constructed example, a <cell nucleus> occupies one <volume (thermodynamics)> unit and the accessible <cytosol> occupies nine. Of $130$ tracer units, $40$ are nuclear and $90$ are cytosolic. With $n_n,n_c$ the amounts and $V_n,V_c$ the accessible <volume (thermodynamics)>, the <concentration> ratio is
$$
C_n=\frac{n_n}{V_n}=40,\qquad C_c=\frac{n_c}{V_c}=10,\qquad R=\frac{C_n}{C_c}=4.
$$
Only $40/130=4/13\approx30.8\%$ of the cargo is nuclear, yet nuclear <concentration> is four times cytosolic <concentration>. A majority outside the <cell nucleus> does not rule out nuclear enrichment.

For comparison, freely exchanging nonbinding cargo at equal <concentrations> would have $n_n=13,n_c=117$: the nuclear fraction would equal the nuclear fraction of accessible <volume (thermodynamics)>, $1/10$. These are constructed amounts, not measurements from the <nuclear pore localization experiment>. Binding, unequal recovery or cargo degradation would require additional accounting before interpreting an assay.

A minimal two-compartment <ordinary differential equation> makes the distinction precise. Assume fixed accessible <volume (thermodynamics)>, no synthesis or degradation, and effective first-order exchange rates $a,b>0$:
$$
\frac{dn_n}{dt}=a n_c-b n_n,\qquad n_c=N-n_n,\qquad \frac{dn_c}{dt}=-\frac{dn_n}{dt}.
$$
Total cargo $N=n_n+n_c$ is conserved. Setting the <derivative> to zero gives
$$
n_n^*=\frac{aN}{a+b},\qquad \frac{C_n^*}{C_c^*}=\frac{aV_c}{bV_n}.
$$
For symmetric passive exchange with conductance $P$, $a=P/V_c$ and $b=P/V_n$, so the <concentration> ratio is one even when the amounts differ. Choosing instead $a=4/9$ and $b=1$ in reciprocal model-time units gives $n_n^*=40$ for $N=130,V_n=1,V_c=9$. The exact <ordinary differential equation> solution is
$$
n_n(t)=n_n^*+\bigl(n_n(0)-n_n^*\bigr)e^{-(a+b)t}.
$$
Substitution verifies the solution, and $a+b>0$ makes departures decay. This effective model can describe an asymmetric exchange balance, but does not by itself identify a <nuclear localization sequence>, transport receptor or source of <energy>.

The usefulness of the <nuclear pore complex> is precisely this combination of separation and exchange: a <cell nucleus> can maintain a distinctive <protein> population while receiving new cargo and exporting <RNA>. The <nuclear pore localization experiment> asks what feature controls entry; the <nuclear pore transport balance> asks what an accumulation readout actually means.

= Nucleolus
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Cell nucleus}

= Cell nucleolus
{synonym}

The <cell nucleolus> is a region inside a <cell nucleus> without a surrounding membrane, where much <ribosomal RNA> is made and processed and <ribosome> subunits begin to assemble with <proteins>. The subunits are exported to the <cytoplasm>; the <cell nucleolus> is not itself a translating <ribosome>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: nucleolus and ribosomal subunits].

= Ribosome
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <ribosome> is the molecular machinery for <biological translation>: it reads <messenger RNA> and joins <amino acids> into a <polypeptide> that can become a <protein>. Both <prokaryotes> and <eukaryotes> have <ribosomes>. A <ribosome> consists of <ribosomal RNA> and <proteins> organized into small and large subunits, with no enclosing membrane. <Ribosomal RNA> contributes to both structure and the <active site> that forms <peptide bonds>; a <ribosome> is therefore not merely a collection of <proteins>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: ribosome composition] and https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/[Nissen et al., The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289, 920–930 (2000)].

\Image[ribosome-translation-schematic.png]
{title=Nuclear <transcription>, <RNA> export and <ribosome>-mediated <biological translation>}
{description=Original schematic of a nuclear <protein>-coding <gene> in a nucleated <eukaryotic> <biological cell>. The upper route tracks <DNA> to <RNA> by <transcription>, <RNA> export, then <biological translation>. The lower example reads three <messenger RNA> <codons> from $5'$ to $3'$ and shows a growing <polypeptide> segment from its <N-terminus> toward its <C-terminus>. Shapes and distances are illustrative; processing, flanking signals, additional machinery and termination are omitted.}

For a computed decoding example, choose the <reading frame> starting at the first displayed <nucleotide> of $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUG\ GCU\ GAA}\text{-}3'$. Under the standard <genetic code>, AUG specifies <methionine> (Met), GCU specifies <alanine> (Ala), and GAA specifies a <glutamic acid residue> (Glu) in the growing <polypeptide>. Nine <nucleotides> divide into three <codons>, giving the ordered three-<amino acid residue> segment Met–Ala–Glu. The <ribosome> reads the <messenger RNA> $5'\to3'$, and the chain grows from its <N-terminus> toward its <C-terminus>. If synthesis begins at this AUG in an appropriate <eukaryotic> initiation context, these are the first three <amino acid residues> before any subsequent processing. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 15.1: standard codon assignments].

For this three-<amino acid residue> segment, joining the three delivered <amino acids> requires two <peptide bonds>, one between Met and Ala and another between Ala and Glu. There is no <stop codon> in the displayed <nucleotide sequence>, so this is a growing segment rather than a complete released <protein>. An isolated nine-<nucleotide> <RNA> is not claimed to supply all signals needed for initiation. This small calculation separates three jobs: the <messenger RNA> specifies order, <transfer RNA> delivers the <amino acids>, and the <ribosome> catalyzes their joining. Each new <amino acid residue> is added at the <C-terminus>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 15.5: initiation and chain elongation].

The beauty of a <ribosome> is its reusable chemistry: the same machinery can assemble many different <proteins> by following different <messenger RNA> templates. The <genetic code> connects an alphabet of <nucleotides> to an alphabet of <amino acids>, while <transfer RNA> links recognition of the message to delivery of building material. Changing the message can therefore change the product without building a different <ribosome> for every <protein>.

In 1955 George E. Palade described small particles in the <cytoplasm>, both free and associated with membranes, in https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2223592/[A small particulate component of the cytoplasm, Journal of Biophysical and Biochemical Cytology 1, 59–68]. The paper documents the structural observations, rather than the full modern decoding mechanism. Complementary biochemical work by Hoagland, Stephenson, Scott, Hecht and Zamecnik described a soluble <RNA> intermediate in <protein> synthesis in 1958, helping establish the role now assigned to <transfer RNA>; see https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13538965/[A soluble ribonucleic acid intermediate in protein synthesis, Journal of Biological Chemistry 231, 241–257]. Together, structural observations and biochemical experiments connected visible cellular machinery with its working parts.

= Ribosome stalling
{parent=Ribosome}

Prolonged delay of a translating <ribosome> at a particular position. Stalls can reduce completed-chain output and cause <ribosome collisions>.

= Peptidyl transfer
{parent=Ribosome}

Formation of a <peptide> bond by transfer of the growing <peptide> to an incoming <aminoacyl-tRNA>. <substrate> sequence can affect the rate independently of decoding accuracy.

= Translation elongation factor P
{parent=Ribosome}

= EF-P
{synonym}
{c}

A bacterial factor that assists peptide-bond formation at susceptible sequences, including consecutive <proline>[prolines]. Functional maturation of the factor and <substrate> sequence affect its activity.<EF-P is essential for rapid synthesis of proteins containing consecutive proline residues by Lili K. Doerfel and others>[Mechanistic substrate tests]{ref}

= Ribosomal protein
{parent=Ribosome}

A <protein> component of a <ribosome>. The machinery can combine products of different <genomes>, especially in an <organelle>.

= Polysome
{parent=Ribosome}

A group of <ribosomes> translating one <messenger RNA>. Occupancy can increase through faster initiation or slower elongation, so the amount of <polysomes> is not by itself a synthesis-rate measurement.

= Ribosomal subunit
{parent=Ribosome}

One of the major assemblies forming a <ribosome>. Small-subunit scanning and reinitiation can persist after release of the large subunit at a short <upstream open reading frame>.

= Ribosome collision
{parent=Ribosome}

Contact between successive <ribosomes> when a following one reaches a stalled or slowly moving predecessor. Collisions can supply regulatory and quality-control signals rather than simply indicating high productive output.

= Gliding bacterium
{parent=Biological cell}

A <gliding bacterium> moves along a surface without swimming through the bulk fluid. In a thin-fluid-film model, its relative motion dissipates energy through <Couette flow>, although the actuation mechanism can involve additional dissipation.

= Neutrophil
{parent=Biological cell}

A <neutrophil> is an immune <biological cell>. Its adhesive rolling on a surface involves contact mechanics, fluid stresses, and binding and unbinding of adhesive bonds.

= Red blood cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A red blood cell is a specialized <biological cell> that transports <oxygen> in blood. Its deformability lets it pass through vessels narrower than its unstressed size. A compliant-body <lubrication> model isolates the coupling between its deformation, the surrounding viscous film and a driving <pressure> difference; it is an idealization rather than a complete membrane or cytoplasm model.

\Include[membrane-potential]{parent=Biological cell}

= Heterokaryon
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <heterokaryon> contains genetically different <cell nuclei> in a shared <cytoplasm>; <cell fusion> can create one.

= Cell fusion
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

<Cell fusion> combines <biological cells> into a shared <cytoplasm> bounded by a common <cell membrane>; it need not immediately fuse their <cell nuclei>.

= Cell cortex
{parent=Biological cell}

The <cell cortex> is a peripheral layer of <cytoplasm> beneath the <cell membrane>, containing the <actomyosin ring> during typical <animal> <cytokinesis>.

= Cell lineage
{parent=Biological cell}

A <cell lineage> follows descent of <biological cells> through successive divisions. <DNA replication> can transmit a <mutation> to descendant <biological cells>.

= Lineage restriction
{parent=Cell lineage}

A narrowing of the descendant <cell types> available to a progenitor. It should be distinguished from failure to sample rare outputs and from changing <developmental competence>.

= Cell of origin
{parent=Cell lineage}

The normal or precursor cellular lineage in which a tumour-initiating evolutionary process occurs. It can differ from the phenotype of the established malignant cells.

= Lineage tracing
{parent=Cell lineage}

Following descendants of identified cells through development. Marker expression at one instant is a state measurement rather than a complete lineage history.

= Germline
{parent=Cell lineage}

The lineage that gives rise to reproductive cells transmitting genetic information between generations, distinguished from somatic body-cell lineages.

= Germline genome defence
{parent=Germline}

Mechanisms protecting the inherited <genome> from <transposable elements> and other threats. Suppression of transposon <RNA>, <chromatin> repression and removal of damaged <cells> are different protective strategies.

= Genetic lineage tracing
{parent=Cell lineage}

Tracking descendants through an inherited genetic label or sequence change. Labelling specificity, timing and independent activation determine what ancestry can be inferred.

= Lineage-tracing dropout
{parent=Genetic lineage tracing}

Failure to recover a lineage label from a sampled descendant. No recovered label is therefore not equivalent to proof of absent ancestry.

= CRISPR lineage recording
{parent=Genetic lineage tracing}
{c}

Using accumulated CRISPR-induced sequence changes as inherited records of ancestry. Nested scars support a lineage tree only after considering recurrent edits, deletion of earlier scars and missing observations.

= scGESTALT
{parent=CRISPR lineage recording}
{c}

A lineage-recording approach coupling inherited CRISPR barcode edits with single-<cell> transcriptomes. It can relate <cell> identity to developmental ancestry while retaining the limitations of incomplete barcode recovery and edit <homoplasy>.<Simultaneous single-cell profiling of lineages and cell types in the vertebrate brain by Bushra Raj and others>[2018, Figures 1 and 3–6, recording pulses and lineage/cell-type recovery]{ref}

= Genetic barcode
{parent=Genetic lineage tracing}

An inherited sequence label used to distinguish <cell> ancestries. Independent acquisition of the same barcode, loss of the readout and nonuniform barcode frequencies can all confound a clonal inference.

= Barcode collision
{parent=Genetic barcode}
{title2=$\sum_jp_j^2$}

Independent <cells> receive the same apparent label. If barcode probabilities are $p_j$, two independent founders match with <probability> $\sum_jp_j^2$, rather than necessarily the inverse of the nominal library size.

= Clonal lineage tracing
{parent=Cell lineage}

<Lineage tracing> that identifies descendants of a single ancestor. A labelled group is informative about a clone only if labelling collisions and independent activation are excluded.

= Clone-size distribution
{parent=Clonal lineage tracing}

The distribution of descendant counts per clone. Differences can arise from proliferation, survival, migration or sampling rather than altered fate choice alone.

= Sparse labelling
{parent=Clonal lineage tracing}

Labelling few enough founder <cells> to resolve individual clones. The acceptable density depends on spatial mixing, label diversity and the chance of independent activation.

= Clonal multipotency
{parent=Clonal lineage tracing}

Production of more than one differentiated <cell type> from one confirmed progenitor. Several separately labelled unipotent <cells> can imitate this pattern if the label is not unique.

= Neutral clonal competition
{parent=Clonal lineage tracing}

Competition among labelled clones with equivalent average reproductive behaviour. Clone extinction can coexist with tissue maintenance, and surviving clones form a selected subset of all initial clones.
